ade-protocol-29-mainnet-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-29-mainnet","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-29-mainnet#check-it-yourself","content":"Entry id `upgrade.protocol-29-mainnet` · compiled from `catalog/upgrade.protocol-29-mainnet.json` · `entry.json`"},"1890":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"page","content":"The testnet ledger that upgraded the protocol to 30","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus","Entries","Config upgrades"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet"},"1891":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","content":"LEDGER_UPGRADE_VERSION 30 on testnet, written by a Galexie node."},"1892":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#the-shape","content":"The shape"},"1893":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#variants","content":"Variants"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#what-a-correct-reader-returns","content":"What a correct reader returns"},"1895":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#check-it-yourself","content":"Check it yourself"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#check-it-yourself","content":"Entry id `upgrade.protocol-30-testnet` · compiled from `catalog/upgrade.protocol-30-testnet.json` · `entry.json`"},"1897":{"id":"/docs/corpus","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"page","content":"The corpus","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/corpus-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"Rare but valid Stellar ledger data, as bytes stellar-core actually emitted, each entry tagged with its CAP and the values a correct reader must return."},"1899":{"id":"/docs/corpus-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#how-an-entry-is-built","content":"How an entry is built"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/corpus-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#picked-from-the-hazard-registry","content":"Picked from the hazard registry"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/corpus-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#written-in-the-catalog-compiled-from-real-runs","content":"Written in the catalog, compiled from real runs"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/corpus-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#facts-and-what-backs-each-one","content":"Facts, and what backs each one"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/corpus-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#verified-on-every-change","content":"Verified on every change"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/corpus-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"Files"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/corpus-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"**What it is.** The heart of XDRProof: real Stellar ledgers in their rarest valid shapes, each kept as the exact bytes stellar-core wrote, with the answers a correct reader must give."},"1906":{"id":"/docs/corpus-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"**Why it matters.** Your code meets ordinary ledgers every day and these rarely, often for the first time in production. The corpus lets you meet them in a test."},"1907":{"id":"/docs/corpus-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"The corpus is a set of **entries**, plus 37 mined ledgers from the real networks. Each entry is one rare shape, such as a signed-payload signer or a trustline created by a contract call, kept as real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes. Each entry also carries the facts a correct reader must find in those bytes."},"1908":{"id":"/docs/corpus-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"Entries grow by purpose, not by volume. Each one names the hazards it targets, and where in the XDR they sit."},"1909":{"id":"/docs/corpus-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"The full list, grouped by family, is on All entries; every entry has its own page."},"1910":{"id":"/docs/corpus-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#picked-from-the-hazard-registry","content":"A hazard is a named way readers break, with its evidence: an upstream issue, a protocol rule or a measurement. An entry targets one or more hazards, and says where each sits in the XDR (its `placement`)."},"1911":{"id":"/docs/corpus-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#written-in-the-catalog-compiled-from-real-runs","content":"The entry's intent and facts are written in its catalog file. The compiler takes the bytes from a recipe run of a bare standalone stellar-core, or from a ledger **mined** from public history, and resolves every fact against them. A fact that doesn't hold fails the build. `make generate` re-runs every recipe twice from scratch and checks that every shipped generated variant is reproduced: the same bytes, or the same content in another change order."},"1912":{"id":"/docs/corpus-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#facts-and-what-backs-each-one","content":"Every expected value says what backs it: `construction` (the recipe set that value), `snapshot` (the SEP-0051 rendering by the pinned stellar-xdr crate), `core` (stellar-core's own decoder), `walker` (XDRProof's schema walker) or `review`.  of the  facts are also confirmed by both stellar-core's decoder and the schema walker; the **Backed by** column on each entry page shows exactly which."},"1913":{"id":"/docs/corpus-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#verified-on-every-change","content":"`make verify` checks the schema, sizes and hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact. `make oracle` checks that each fact's values also appear in what stellar-core's own decoder and the schema walker read."},"1914":{"id":"/docs/corpus-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"<id>.json\" />"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/corpus-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"entry.json\" />"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/corpus-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"p30-mainnet-generated.xdr\" />"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/corpus-19","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"p28-testnet-generated.xdr\" />"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/corpus-20","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"manifest.json\" />"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/corpus-21","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"entry.schema.json\" />"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/corpus-22","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"`manifest.json` is generated from the entries, one row per variant. `make verify` fails if it is out of date."},"1921":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","type":"page","content":"Mined ledgers","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus","Real network data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/mined"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"37 real mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, for shapes a standalone generator cannot produce, or that are worth having as real network bytes: eviction at the cap, settings votes, a mainnet freeze, protocol-12 V0 envelopes."},"1923":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/mined#every-mined-shape","content":"Every mined shape"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"**What it is.** Real mainnet and testnet ledgers, downloaded from the public data lake with their hashes and the query that found them."},"1925":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"**Why it matters.** Some shapes only happen on a live network, such as eviction at the per-ledger cap or a validator vote. These are the real bytes, not a reconstruction."},"1926":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"Some shapes only happen on a real network, and some are worth having as real network bytes. Examples:"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"eviction at the per-ledger cap;"},"1928":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"validator settings votes;"},"1929":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"a mainnet freeze of ledger keys (CAP-0077);"},"1930":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"protocol-12 V0 envelopes;"},"1931":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"protocol-22 refunds;"},"1932":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"multi-stage parallel Soroban phases."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"`mined/` holds those as real `LedgerCloseMeta`, fetched from SDF's public data lake and located with BigQuery over the public Hubble dataset. The queries are in `mined/queries/`."},"1934":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"| File                                        | What it is                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mined/ledgers/<network>-<seq>.lcm.xdr.zst` | One ledger, zstd-compressed. Its sha256 and size describe the decompressed bytes: the lake object minus its 12-byte batch header |","table":"table-0"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"| File               | What it is                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mined/index.json` | One record per shape found in a ledger: the lake URL, the stellar-core version that wrote it, how it was located, the findings with exact JSON paths, and why readers break on it |","table":"table-0"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"`make verify` also runs `tools/mined-verify.mjs`. Every file must match its hash, round-trip byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present. `make coverage` reports the union arms these ledgers reach: ** of **."},"1937":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"<Callout title=\"Network data, not entries\">These ledgers were written by SDF's Galexie with its default flags, so their metadata differs from a default node's (see Found while building). Most are not corpus entries: entries carry facts and hazards, and these carry findings. The catalog also compiles entries from them: entries so far, built from of these ledgers, the signed-payload signer among them.</Callout>"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"page","content":"Recipes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus","Making entries"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"Deterministic scripts that drive a bare standalone stellar-core to emit rare shapes: fixed seeds, fixed close times, no RPC, no network, no funds."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"The two recipe sets"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"The test contracts"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"How they are run"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**What it is.** Small scripts that make a private, offline stellar-core produce each rare shape on demand."},"1944":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Why it matters.** A shape you can rebuild from scratch is one anyone can verify, and one that can be made again when the protocol changes."},"1945":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"A recipe is a Node script (using `@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0) that drives one fresh **standalone stellar-core** over its HTTP port:"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Fixed seeds.** Every keypair is `sha256('xdrproof:' + name)`; the root account comes from the passphrase."},"1947":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Fixed time.** Ledger n closes at `1767225600 + n × 30 days`."},"1948":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**No RPC, no simulation.** Transactions go straight to core's `tx` endpoint, and ledgers close with `manualclose`."},"1949":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Ledger 2 is always the protocol upgrade,** and never an entry."},"1950":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"Core's metadata stream is split into one `ledger-NNN.xdr` file per ledger, plus `SHA256SUMS`. An entry is one of those ledgers, chosen because it holds a rare shape."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"| Set     | Script                    | Ledgers               | What it covers                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------- | ------------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Classic | `recipes/classic/gen.mjs` | 3–105, at protocol 30 | Basics (the signed-payload signers at ledger 6), a catalogue of operations, two failure suites, blueprint placements, maximum-length vectors, a revocation cascade, surge pricing, and a final base-fee, set-size and ledger-flags upgrade |","table":"table-0"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"| Set     | Script                        | Ledgers     | What it covers                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Soroban | `recipes/soroban/gen/gen.mjs` | 3–94 at P30 | Blueprint placements, apply-time failures, fee-bump inner failures, forged and V2-rendered auth signatures, two test contracts, delegated custom accounts, external-ref executables, config upgrades, frozen keys, eviction and automatic restore |","table":"table-0"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"Entries are compiled from recipe steps (and mined ledgers) by the catalog:  so far. The hazard registry references recipe ledgers by number."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"| Contract       | SDK                | What it does                                                                                        |\n| -------------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `xdrproof-kit` | soroban-sdk 25.3.0 | `put(k, v)` stores a value, emits it and returns it; `nest` builds authorization trees of any depth |","table":"table-1"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"| Contract        | SDK                | What it does                                                                                                                              |\n| --------------- | ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `xdrproof-kit2` | soroban-sdk 28.0.0 | A constructor, persistent, temporary and instance storage, CAP-0085 executable references, and a `__check_auth` that delegates (CAP-0071) |","table":"table-1"},"1956":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"Both ship as prebuilt Wasm next to their source."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"Each run starts the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0. The core config sets manual close, a fixed node seed, and one **emitter-flag profile** from `recipes/flags/`:"},"1958":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-19","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile | Flags set                                                                                      | Who runs this way             |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `base`  | `EMIT_CLASSIC_EVENTS`, `EMIT_SOROBAN_TRANSACTION_META_EXT_V1`, `EMIT_LEDGER_CLOSE_META_EXT_V1` | the corpus's shipped variants |","table":"table-2"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-20","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile   | Flags set                                                                                                                  | Who runs this way                                                  |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `galexie` | all six: the three above plus `BACKFILL_STELLAR_ASSET_EVENTS`, `BACKFILL_RESTORE_META`, `ENABLE_SOROBAN_DIAGNOSTIC_EVENTS` | Galexie's default config, which public data lakes are written with |","table":"table-2"},"1960":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-21","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile | Flags set | Who runs this way                       |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `bare`  | none      | a node on stellar-core's example config |","table":"table-2"},"1961":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-22","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"Soroban runs also set `TESTING_SOROBAN_HIGH_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`. Variant keys carry the profile as a suffix when it is not `base`."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-23","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"`make generate` runs 10 configurations twice each: classic P30 on both passphrases, Soroban P30 and P28 on both passphrases, and the galexie and bare profiles at P30 mainnet for each lane. It checks that every shipped generated variant is reproduced. See Generate."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"page","content":"Manifest schema","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema"},"1964":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"Every field of an entry, generated from entry.schema.json (JSON Schema 2020-12), with how the RFP's required fields map onto it."},"1965":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"The RFP's fields"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#entry","content":"Entry"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#variant","content":"Variant"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#fact","content":"Fact"},"1969":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#sdk-check","content":"SDK check"},"1970":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"**What it is.** The format of an entry, field by field, as a JSON Schema."},"1971":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"**Why it matters.** Any tool, in any language, can load the corpus and know what each field means without reading XDRProof's code."},"1972":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"The schema is `schema/v1/entry.schema.json`, version 1.1. Every table on this page is generated from it."},"1973":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema                                            |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| Entry ID  | `id` + `variants[].key` (permanent; never reused) |","table":"table-0"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema |\n| --------- | ------ |\n| CAP       | `caps` |","table":"table-0"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field        | Schema                |\n| ---------------- | --------------------- |\n| Protocol version | `variants[].protocol` |","table":"table-0"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema                                                                                                                    |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Source    | `variants[].source`: `mined` with the lake URL and sha256, or `generated` with the core version, emitter flags and recipe |","table":"table-0"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field      | Schema                                              |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Synthetic flag | `variants[].synthetic` (with `emittable` when true) |","table":"table-0"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field               | Schema                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Expected decoded values | `expected.facts` (order-independent where needed) and `expected.sdk_checks` |","table":"table-0"},"1979":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"Beyond those, each entry names its `hazards` (with the `placement` in the XDR), and each variant its `focus` transaction. Variants may be zstd-compressed (large mainnet ledgers); `size` and `sha256` describe the decompressed bytes."},"1980":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#variant","content":"One ledger file of an entry: a protocol, a network passphrase, and how it was obtained."},"1981":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#fact","content":"One value a correct reader must find, at a path in the SEP-0051 JSON rendering. It is given as `equals`, `length` or `some` (a change that matches, in any order)."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#sdk-check","content":"A rule for an SDK helper rather than a value. For example, a native projection must keep every entry of a map or refuse it."},"1983":{"id":"/docs/findings","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"page","content":"Found while building","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings"},"1984":{"id":"/docs/findings-0","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"What building the corpus measured about stellar-core, its metadata and the decoders. Each one is a hazard for indexers."},"1985":{"id":"/docs/findings-1","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"stellar-core emits some operation changes in a random order"},"1986":{"id":"/docs/findings-2","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"Node flags change the metadata, never the ledger"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/findings-3","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-ledger-written-with-diagnostic-events-on-is-not-byte-reproducible","content":"A Soroban ledger written with diagnostic events on is not byte-reproducible"},"1988":{"id":"/docs/findings-4","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#the-public-data-lake-carries-transactionmeta-v4-even-for-2019-ledgers","content":"The public data lake carries TransactionMeta v4, even for 2019 ledgers"},"1989":{"id":"/docs/findings-5","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-fee-source-merged-earlier-in-the-same-ledger-gets-no-refund","content":"A Soroban fee source merged earlier in the same ledger gets no refund"},"1990":{"id":"/docs/findings-6","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#stellar-cores-own-golden-ledgers-reach-254-of-499-arms","content":"stellar-core's own golden ledgers reach 254 of 499 arms"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/findings-7","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#soroban-authorization-trees-apply-up-to-245-levels-deep","content":"Soroban authorization trees apply up to 245 levels deep"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/findings-8","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#the-wasm-build-of-rs-stellar-xdr-cannot-decode-those-ledgers","content":"The WASM build of rs-stellar-xdr cannot decode those ledgers"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/findings-9","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"A create-contract authorization is signed over bytes it does not contain"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/findings-10","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#codes-read-as-submission-only-appear-in-closed-ledgers","content":"Codes read as submission-only appear in closed ledgers"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/findings-11","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#order-across-ledger-keys-is-unstable-the-pairing-for-one-key-is-stable","content":"Order across ledger keys is unstable; the pairing for one key is stable"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/findings-12","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"**What it is.** What building the corpus measured about stellar-core and the decoders: things the documentation doesn't say."},"1997":{"id":"/docs/findings-13","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"**Why it matters.** Each one is a trap for anyone reading the ledger, and each is now a test in the corpus."},"1998":{"id":"/docs/findings-14","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"Each finding says where it comes from: a committed measurement, a mined ledger, or an observation on recipe runs. The recipe runs themselves are not shipped. The recipes are, along with the order-scan and flag-profile results."},"1999":{"id":"/docs/findings-15","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"Across  fresh runs of each recipe lane,  of  classic recipe ledgers and  of  Soroban ones came out in more than one byte order (the protocol-upgrade ledger aside, which varies too). Their headers and content were identical.  classic ledgers () had a different order in every run. The cause is hash-map iteration with a per-process random salt. Measured by `make order-scan`; the results are committed in `recipes/*/order-scan/`."},"2000":{"id":"/docs/findings-16","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/findings-17","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"The recipes were run under the three flag profiles (base, Galexie and bare). Every ledger of both lanes keeps the same header hash."},"2002":{"id":"/docs/findings-18","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"**Bare** changes the metadata content of every ledger except the protocol-upgrade one. There are no classic events, no fee events, and v0 meta extensions."},"2003":{"id":"/docs/findings-19","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"**Galexie's** extra flags change no classic ledger, and 46 Soroban ones: every ledger with a contract call. In those, each contract event appears twice, in the operation's events and again among the diagnostic events."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/findings-20","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"`make generate` re-measures this into `recipes/<lane>/profiles/`."},"2005":{"id":"/docs/findings-21","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-ledger-written-with-diagnostic-events-on-is-not-byte-reproducible","content":"Every call carries a `core_metrics` diagnostic event, `invoke_time_nsecs`: the wall-clock time the host spent. Two nodes running Galexie's default config write different bytes for the same ledger. The corpus's normalised form zeroes that one value."},"2006":{"id":"/docs/findings-22","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#the-public-data-lake-carries-transactionmeta-v4-even-for-2019-ledgers","content":"Every lake object inspected, at protocols 12, 22 and 29, has v4 meta. The protocol-12 mainnet ledger in `mined/` has v4 meta and 532 V0 envelopes. This is because Galexie sets `BACKFILL_STELLAR_ASSET_EVENTS`, which makes stellar-core write v4 at any protocol. A default node replaying the same ledger writes v2 (v3 for protocols 20–22). Only the `LedgerCloseMeta` arm varies by era: v0 at protocol 12, v1 at 22, v2 at 29."},"2007":{"id":"/docs/findings-23","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-fee-source-merged-earlier-in-the-same-ledger-gets-no-refund","content":"`fee_charged` is then the full declared maximum, with no refund change and no refund event. A reader that computes the fee as the charge minus the refund, or that expects a refund for every Soroban transaction, gets it wrong."},"2008":{"id":"/docs/findings-24","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-cores-own-golden-ledgers-reach-254-of-499-arms","content":"A `LedgerCloseMeta` reader can meet 499 XDR union arms at v30.0. stellar-core's own golden ledger files reach 254 of them. For comparison, the corpus entries reach  and the mined ledgers  (Coverage). Many of the missing arms are result codes that never appear in a closed ledger. Measured by `make coverage-goldens` against stellar-core commit `f2b91947`."},"2009":{"id":"/docs/findings-25","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#soroban-authorization-trees-apply-up-to-245-levels-deep","content":"At Protocol 30, an authorization tree 245 levels deep applies. At 246 the transaction is included, but traps. Any indexer cap below about 246 meets real, valid ledgers. Re-checked by `make verify` on the entries `auth.tree-depth-245-max` and `auth.tree-depth-246-trapped`."},"2010":{"id":"/docs/findings-26","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#the-wasm-build-of-rs-stellar-xdr-cannot-decode-those-ledgers","content":"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM, refuses  corpus variants that stellar-core wrote (`depth limit exceeded`): 8 whose authorization tree is 245 levels deep, the deepest that applies, and 5 at 246, which core includes and traps. The package hard-codes `XDR_MAX_DEPTH = 500`, set to mirror the Soroban host's budget limit, and applies it to the whole `LedgerCloseMeta`, whose path down to the auth tree is deeper. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0, go-stellar-sdk v0.9.0, both Python decoders and native rs-stellar-xdr read them. This is finding F-1, reported as js-stellar-xdr-json#67; see Reported upstream."},"2011":{"id":"/docs/findings-27","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"A valid v1 create-contract authorization is signed over its V2 rendering. The host reads `SOROBAN_AUTHORIZED_FUNCTION_TYPE_CREATE_CONTRACT_HOST_FN` into its V2 form and hashes the signature payload from the `CREATE_CONTRACT_V2_HOST_FN` rendering (soroban-env-host `auth.rs`, as stellar-core v30 links it at protocols 28 and 30). Signed over its own bytes, as js-stellar-base's `authorizeEntry` signs it, the entry fails ed25519 verification; signed over the V2 rendering, the contract is created."},"2012":{"id":"/docs/findings-28","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"This is by design since protocol 22, to keep one valid payload per action (stellar discussion #1501). Transactions built through RPC simulation get it right; offline signers must sign the V2 rendering. A verifier that recomputes the payload from the entry rejects a signature core accepted (hazard H-B16). `tools/auth-payload-check.mjs` re-checks it with hand-encoded preimages, no SDK."},"2013":{"id":"/docs/findings-29","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#codes-read-as-submission-only-appear-in-closed-ledgers","content":"Core re-checks sequence numbers, signatures and preconditions at apply. So a classic transaction earlier in the same ledger can make a queued Soroban transaction fail with `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` (a signature needed at submission becomes surplus once a master weight rises), and fee-bump inner transactions fail with `txBAD_SEQ`, `txNO_ACCOUNT`, `txBAD_AUTH`, `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` or `txBAD_MIN_SEQ_AGE_OR_GAP` inside `txFEE_BUMP_INNER_FAILED`. The corpus's failed-transaction family holds them."},"2014":{"id":"/docs/findings-30","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#order-across-ledger-keys-is-unstable-the-pairing-for-one-key-is-stable","content":"Between core processes, the order across different ledger keys changes. The STATE → UPDATED pairing for one key does not. Readers can rely on the pairing, never on the order between keys."},"2015":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"page","content":"Reported upstream","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-0","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"Two bugs XDRProof's runs found that nobody had reported, now filed upstream with a figure and a reproduction: a lost refund in wallet-backend and a decoder that refuses a ledger stellar-core closed. And a tested fix offered on an issue that was already open."},"2017":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-1","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"F-3: balances stored short on protocol 20–22 ledgers"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-2","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"F-1: a decoder refuses a ledger stellar-core closed"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-3","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"wallet-backend#820: an authorization walk with no limit"},"2020":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-4","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"Found, not filed"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-5","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"**What it is.** Two bugs XDRProof found in other projects' code that nobody had reported, filed upstream, and a tested fix offered on a third that was already open."},"2022":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-6","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"**Why it matters.** A test kit earns trust by what it catches. Each report below comes with a figure, a reproduction anyone can run, and, where we could, a tested fix. Filing is done the way the projects ask: a duplicate search first, one issue per bug."},"2023":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-7","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"Before protocol 23, stellar-core credits a Soroban fee refund inside the transaction's own changes. wallet-backend builds native balances from fees, operations and the newer, separate refund block only, so every refunded account on such a ledger is stored short by exactly its refund, while wallet-backend's own balance history adds up."},"2024":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-8","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"It bites when an indexer works through older ledgers, such as a catch-up from the data lake. The fix is on a branch; the whole corpus replayed into it handles both variants of that ledger and changes no other verdict."},"2025":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-9","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, the WebAssembly build of rs-stellar-xdr, limits nesting to 500 levels for the whole value it decodes. stellar-core allows 500 per authorization entry, and the layers around a transaction add more, so the deepest trees core applies cannot be decoded once they are inside a transaction or a ledger. The corpus made them on a standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 with Soroban's resource limits raised for testing (`TESTING_SOROBAN_HIGH_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`), which the entry records."},"2026":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-10","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"It refuses  corpus variants that stellar-core wrote. Every other decoder tested reads them; see Conformance."},"2027":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-11","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"wallet-backend walks the authorization tree a transaction declares with no depth or node limit (issue #820, filed by another team). The linter finds it as `XP002/walk-unbudgeted`."},"2028":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-12","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"The fix offered bounds the walk well above anything the network applies today, and stops at the limit instead of failing. Our first version failed at depth 64, which would have halted ingestion on the 245-deep ledgers in the corpus; that mistake is now a linter rule of its own, `XP002/budget-fails`."},"2029":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-13","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"| ID  | What                                                                                                     | Why not filed                                                                                |\n| --- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| F-2 | The Python SDK, `stellar-sdk` 17.0.1, raises on a muxed contract address (CAP-0084) when rendering JSON. | Already fixed on its main branch by the switch to `stellar-xdr`; `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 passes. |","table":"table-0"},"2030":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-14","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"| ID  | What                                                                                                                  | Why not filed                                                                                                    |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| F-4 | wallet-backend's Blend decoder uses an arbitrary `ScAddress` value as an account (found by `XP001/muxed-as-account`). | Not reachable today, and the Stellar contribution guide asks not to file speculative findings. The fix is ready. |","table":"table-0"},"2031":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-15","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"Bugs that would let anyone halt or crash an indexer are reported privately first, through SDF's security channel, and are not published here until a fix ships or 90 days pass (`findings/policy.md`). The full triage is `findings/REPORT.md`."},"2032":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"page","content":"Upstream bugs","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced"},"2033":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-0","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"Real bugs in wallet-backend and the SDKs, reproduced from corpus ledgers and test vectors, plus what the runs found that nobody had reported. Each is pinned, so a fix upstream shows up as an unexpected pass."},"2034":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-1","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-before-and-after-each-fix","content":"wallet-backend, before and after each fix"},"2035":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-2","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-main-still-open","content":"wallet-backend main, still open"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-3","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#found-by-xdrproof","content":"Found by XDRProof"},"2037":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-4","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"In the SDKs"},"2038":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-5","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"**What it is.** Real bugs that other teams found in wallet-backend and the SDKs, reproduced from corpus ledgers and test vectors."},"2039":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-6","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"**Why it matters.** Each one is pinned. If a fix regresses, or an old bug turns up in another SDK, the run fails."},"2040":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-7","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"Verdicts come from state, never from log text: the indexer's cursor, process and stored rows for replay, the value returned for conformance. Every row below is pinned in `replay/expectations/` or `conformance/expectations/`, so a fix upstream shows up as an unexpected pass and the row is updated. The full triage is findings/REPORT.md."},"2041":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-8","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-before-and-after-each-fix","content":"Run by `make replay-sightings`: each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix. See Replay."},"2042":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-9","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-main-still-open","content":"The whole corpus replayed into `main` ():  of  variants handled,  halted. The rest:"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-10","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#found-by-xdrproof","content":"What the runs found that nobody had reported is on its own page, with figures and reproductions: Reported upstream."},"2044":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-11","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"Run by `make conformance`: the same corpus and vectors through every build. See Conformance."},"2045":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-12","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                               | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Assets ordered by strkey text instead of XDR bytes | , fixed in 17.2.1 |","table":"table-0"},"2046":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-13","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                   | What           | Upstream        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | --------------- |\n| `go-stellar-sdk` v0.7.1 | The #1766 twin | fixed in v0.7.2 |","table":"table-0"},"2047":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-14","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                                                                 | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Muxed IDs and offer IDs accepted in non-canonical forms; offer IDs past 2^53 rounded | , fixed in 17.3.0 |","table":"table-0"},"2048":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-15","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                                         | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Map keys that collide on native conversion, silently dropped | , fixed in 17.3.0 |","table":"table-0"},"2049":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-16","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                        | Upstream        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | `scval.to_native` keeps 2 of 5 colliding map keys, silently | , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2050":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-17","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                                | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | Accepts non-zero XDR padding, an optional flag of 2 and a bool of 2 | , , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2051":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-18","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                                                                                                                                                            | Upstream        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | SEP-0051 JSON differs in four places (result codes, `ClawbackOp.from_`, `AllowTrustOp.asset`, `contract_id` as hex). With those four allowed, its JSON equals rs-stellar-xdr's on every variant | , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2052":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-19","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                                    | What                                                                              | Upstream |\n| ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `rs-stellar-xdr` 30.0.0, native and WASM | A bool of 2 decodes as `false` and re-encodes as 0; stellar-core reads it as true | , open   |","table":"table-0"},"2053":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-20","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                   | What                                                                                          | Upstream |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0 | `strkey.DecodeAny` accepts SEP-0023's invalid claimable-balance key with a non-zero type byte | , , open |","table":"table-0"},"2054":{"id":"/docs/hazards","page_id":"/docs/hazards","type":"page","content":"The hazard registry","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards"},"2055":{"id":"/docs/hazards-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"Every named way readers of the Stellar ledger break, in eight layers, each with its evidence. The corpus grows by covering them."},"2056":{"id":"/docs/hazards-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"How to read a hazard"},"2057":{"id":"/docs/hazards-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"**What it is.** Every known way a program reading the Stellar ledger goes wrong, in  layers, each with its evidence."},"2058":{"id":"/docs/hazards-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"**Why it matters.** It is the corpus's blueprint: each entry targets hazards from this list, and each hazard says which check exercises it."},"2059":{"id":"/docs/hazards-4","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"`caps/registry.json` is the blueprint for the corpus. Each **hazard** is a named way a reader of the Stellar ledger breaks. Some examples:"},"2060":{"id":"/docs/hazards-5","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a muxed account stored without its ID;"},"2061":{"id":"/docs/hazards-6","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a contract call that creates a trustline with no event;"},"2062":{"id":"/docs/hazards-7","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a failed transaction that still changes state;"},"2063":{"id":"/docs/hazards-8","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a valid ledger a decoder refuses."},"2064":{"id":"/docs/hazards-9","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"Every hazard carries its **evidence**: an upstream issue, a protocol rule or a measurement. Every corpus entry names the hazards it targets."},"2065":{"id":"/docs/hazards-10","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field | What it says                              |\n| ----- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `id`  | `H-` + layer letter + number, e.g. `H-A3` |","table":"table-0"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/hazards-11","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field   | What it says             |\n| ------- | ------------------------ |\n| `title` | The failure, in one line |","table":"table-0"},"2067":{"id":"/docs/hazards-12","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field      | What it says                                                           |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `evidence` | Where it was seen: issues, pull requests, protocol rules, measurements |","table":"table-0"},"2068":{"id":"/docs/hazards-13","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field  | What it says                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `test` | What the corpus holds or will hold for it. `C5` and `S11` are classic and Soroban recipe ledgers; `c-base` and `s-base` name a recipe step; `M:` names a shape in the mined mainnet ledgers (`mined/index.json`); `todo` marks a placement not yet built |","table":"table-0"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/hazards-14","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field    | What it says                                                                                                |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `checks` | Which check will exercise it: replay probe, conformance, linter, guide, verify facts (the guide is planned) |","table":"table-0"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/hazards-15","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"The **covered** column lists the hazards an entry already targets. The rest are the corpus's to-do list, in the open."},"2071":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","type":"page","content":"Layer A: Addresses and identities","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"Every hazard in layer A of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"Muxed accounts, signed-payload, hash-x and pre-auth signers, contract, liquidity-pool and claimable-balance addresses, and fee-bump sources: the places where an address is longer, or stranger, than a G key."},"2074":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"**Why it matters.** An address is the key an indexer files everything under. When it is longer or stranger than a G… key, rows land under the wrong account, or the insert fails."},"2075":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2076":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","type":"page","content":"Layer B: Values and encodings","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b"},"2077":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"Every hazard in layer B of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2078":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"Asset ordering, map keys that collide, amounts past 2⁵³, wide integers, invalid UTF-8, maximum lengths, nesting depth, and an authorization signed over bytes it does not contain: values that change, or break, when they are converted, decoded or verified."},"2079":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"**Why it matters.** A value that changes when it is converted is a wrong number in front of a user: an amount rounded, a map key lost, a signature that won't verify."},"2080":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","type":"page","content":"Layer C: Transaction lifecycle and results","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c"},"2082":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"Every hazard in layer C of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"Failed transactions that still change state, fee bumps, sponsorship, Soroban failures, order-book matching, liquidity pools, claimable balances, and fees that differ from what was bid."},"2084":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"**Why it matters.** Whether a transaction succeeded, what it cost and what it changed are three different questions, and the ledger answers each in a different place."},"2085":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2086":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","type":"page","content":"Layer D: State changes no operation explains","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d"},"2087":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"Every hazard in layer D of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2088":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"Ledger entries created, updated or removed by something other than an operation that names them: trustlines from contract calls, eviction, restores, revocation."},"2089":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"**Why it matters.** A reader that only looks at operations misses state that changed for other reasons, and its balances drift away from the ledger."},"2090":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","type":"page","content":"Layer E: Events","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e"},"2092":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"Every hazard in layer E of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2093":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"Unified events (CAP-0067), event data carrying muxed IDs and deep maps, and diagnostic events emitted twice."},"2094":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"**Why it matters.** Events are the easiest way to follow contract activity, and the easiest to over-trust: some repeat, some carry deep data, some come from failed calls."},"2095":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2096":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","type":"page","content":"Layer F: Ledger level","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f"},"2097":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Every hazard in layer F of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2098":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Upgrade ledgers, empty and very large ledgers, parallel Soroban stages, change order that varies between core processes, and the same ledger in different bytes."},"2099":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"**Why it matters.** Code tested on ordinary ledgers meets the unusual ones at upgrades and peaks: empty, huge, reordered or upgraded ledgers."},"2100":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","type":"page","content":"Layer G: Protocol and network variants","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g"},"2102":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Every hazard in layer G of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Bytes that depend on the network passphrase, older protocols, mainnet-only code paths in core, and CAPs gated before mainnet."},"2104":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"**Why it matters.** The same transaction can be different bytes on another network or protocol. Code that assumes one of them breaks on the other."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2106":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","type":"page","content":"Layer H: Reader robustness (SDK-only)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h"},"2107":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Every hazard in layer H of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2108":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Depth bombs, over-length vectors, truncated input and non-canonical encodings that an SDK must reject safely."},"2109":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"**Why it matters.** An SDK is the first thing to touch untrusted bytes. It must refuse bad input cleanly; crashing, or quietly accepting it, passes the problem on."},"2110":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2111":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"page","content":"Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture"},"2112":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture","content":"How the parts fit: where the ledgers come from, where 'correct' comes from, the three tools that run the corpus against real code, and what the Stellar Community Fund grant would fund."},"2113":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"Built today, and what the grant funds"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"The RFP's deliverables, mapped"},"2115":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"Where the ledgers come from"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"Where \"correct\" comes from"},"2117":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#three-tools-that-run-it-against-real-code","content":"Three tools that run it against real code"},"2118":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#replay-for-indexers","content":"Replay, for indexers"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#conformance-for-sdks","content":"Conformance, for SDKs"},"2120":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-linter-for-indexer-source","content":"The linter, for indexer source"},"2121":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#findings-and-disclosure","content":"Findings and disclosure"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci-funded","content":"Distribution and CI (funded)"},"2123":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#protocol-upgrades","content":"Protocol upgrades"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-12","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#independence","content":"Independence"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-13","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#security","content":"Security"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-14","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"Technology"},"2127":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-15","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture","content":"**What it is.** A map of XDRProof: the corpus in the middle, the checks that prove it, and three tools that run it against real code: replay for indexers, conformance for SDKs, and a linter for indexer source."},"2128":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-16","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture","content":"**Why it matters.** Everything marked built here exists in the repository today and re-runs with one command, so you can check it instead of trusting it. What the grant would fund is listed separately."},"2129":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-17","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                 | State | Re-run it     |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ------------- |\n| Manifest schema (JSON Schema 2020-12) with every field the RFP names | built | `make verify` |","table":"table-0"},"2130":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-18","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                 | State | Re-run it            |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | -------------------- |\n| Hazard registry:  named ways readers of the ledger break, in  layers | built | `caps/registry.json` |","table":"table-0"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-19","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                       | State | Re-run it                      |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------------------ |\n| Corpus:  entries,  variants, from a pinned stellar-core v30.0.0 and SDF's public data lake | built | `make verify`, `make generate` |","table":"table-0"},"2132":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-20","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                         | State | Re-run it     |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ------------- |\n| Independent readers: stellar-core's own decoder and a schema walker confirm  expected values | built | `make oracle` |","table":"table-0"},"2133":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-21","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                          | State | Re-run it              |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ---------------------- |\n| Coverage:  of  union arms reached; the other  each classified with a stellar-core source line | built | `make coverage-report` |","table":"table-0"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-22","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                                         | State | Re-run it          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------ |\n| SDK conformance runner:  SDK builds on the corpus and test vectors,  older releases on fixed-bug regressions | built | `make conformance` |","table":"table-0"},"2135":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-23","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                  | State | Re-run it     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ------------- |\n| Indexer replay harness, with a wallet-backend adapter | built | `make replay` |","table":"table-0"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-24","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                 | State | Re-run it                         |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | --------------------------------- |\n| `xplint`, a Go linter for the three ingestion mistakes the RFP names | built | `make lint-selftest`, `make lint` |","table":"table-0"},"2137":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-25","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                       | State    | Re-run it         |\n| -------------------------- | -------- | ----------------- |\n| Findings reported upstream | reported | Reported upstream |","table":"table-0"},"2138":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-26","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"Everything above is pre-award work. The $60,000 award funds 120 engineer-days through the v1.0 release:"},"2139":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-27","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"the indexer-hardening guide, reviewed by a stellar-core or platform engineer;"},"2140":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-28","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"per-CAP and golden-agreement reports, and a memory gate on replay;"},"2141":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-29","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"packages for JavaScript, Go and Rust, CI workflows and a GitHub Action;"},"2142":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-30","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"two more indexer adapters, and runners for five more SDKs;"},"2143":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-31","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"this documentation site."},"2144":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-32","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"After v1.0, XDRProof is maintained for 18 months, outside the award budget."},"2145":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-33","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                                 | XDRProof                                                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A corpus of real and synthetic XDR shapes, each tagged with its CAP and protocol | `entries/` and `manifest.json`; each variant records its protocol, its source and whether it is synthetic |","table":"table-1"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-34","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                       | XDRProof                                                                                                                              |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Expected decoded values for each entry | each entry's facts, every one backed by construction, a snapshot, stellar-core, the walker or review; none rests on one decoder alone |","table":"table-1"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-35","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                      | XDRProof                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A coverage report per CAP and per arm | `COVERAGE.md` per arm and per hazard today; per CAP is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-36","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                           | XDRProof                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Agreement with stellar-core's golden files | `make coverage-goldens` compares arm coverage today; a comparison of decoded field paths and values at a pinned core revision is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-37","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                   | XDRProof                                                                    |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A replay harness for indexers, with bounded memory | `replay/` with a documented adapter contract; a memory gate in CI is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-38","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                           | XDRProof                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A linter for the three ingestion mistakes, clean on current wallet-backend | `linter/` catches all three. On unmodified wallet-backend `main` it reports  findings, all true, and  with XDRProof's two tested patches, offered upstream. Until an unmodified current revision runs clean, this check stays open |","table":"table-1"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-39","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                    | XDRProof                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| An indexer-hardening guide, reviewed by a core or platform engineer | funded, published only after that review |","table":"table-1"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-40","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for        | XDRProof          |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------- |\n| Findings filed upstream | Reported upstream |","table":"table-1"},"2153":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-41","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Made on a bare stellar-core.** Recipes drive a standalone stellar-core v30.0.0, pinned by image digest, with no network, no keys and no funds. `make generate` rebuilds every run twice from scratch and checks that each pair agrees.  entries come from here."},"2154":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-42","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Order is measured, not assumed.** stellar-core writes some changes in an order that varies between runs. `make order-scan` runs each recipe lane  times and records which ledgers vary; the corpus ships the rarer orderings as bytes, so a reader that depends on order fails."},"2155":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-43","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Mined from the real networks.** `mined/` keeps  mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, with their URLs and hashes, for shapes that are cheaper to find than to make.  entries come only from mined ledgers;  from both."},"2156":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"Expected values never come from the SDK under test."},"2157":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make verify` round-trips every variant, byte for byte, through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0 and checks every fact."},"2158":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make oracle` confirms each checkable value with two readers that share no code with any SDK: stellar-core's own C++ decoder and XDRProof's schema walker."},"2159":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make novelty` checks that no entry is redundant: over  atoms,  entries hold something no other entry does, and the other  differ by where the hazard sits."},"2160":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#replay-for-indexers","content":"Each corpus ledger is served to a real indexer build through a mock stellar-rpc, in a fresh session with its own database. The verdict comes from what the indexer stored, never from its logs. An indexer joins with a small `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands; wallet-backend is the reference. On `main`,  of  variants are handled and  halt. Replay"},"2161":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#conformance-for-sdks","content":"Each SDK build is wrapped in a small program that speaks one line-delimited JSON protocol, so a new language needs one small program. Every known difference is pinned with its cause, so a new failure and a fix upstream both fail the run until the expectation is updated. Conformance"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-linter-for-indexer-source","content":"`xplint` is a set of Go analyzers, one per mistake the RFP names. It writes text, JSON and SARIF for GitHub code scanning, and it fires before each upstream fix of these mistakes and goes quiet after. Linter"},"2163":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#findings-and-disclosure","content":"Ordinary bugs are written up and reported upstream after a duplicate search, each with a figure, a reproduction and, where possible, a tested fix. Halts and crashes anyone can trigger are reported privately first, through SDF's security channel, and are not published until a fix ships or 90 days pass."},"2164":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci-funded","content":"**npm:** `xdrproof`, the command-line tool, and `@xdrproof/corpus`, the corpus with a loader and test helpers for Vitest and Jest."},"2165":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci-funded","content":"**Go** and **Rust:** the corpus as a Go module with a table-test helper, and a Rust loader crate."},"2166":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci-funded","content":"**Workflows** with actions pinned by SHA and read-only permissions: check, conformance, replay, determinism, golden agreement, an upstream watch that opens a pull request when stellar-xdr, stellar-core or an SDK tags a release, and release with checksums and provenance."},"2167":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci-funded","content":"**A GitHub Action**, `XDRProof/action`, that runs the checks on a pinned corpus in another team's CI and uploads SARIF."},"2168":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#protocol-upgrades","content":"stellar-xdr's definitions are the input. A new arm, enum member or CAP shows up as unclassified coverage and fails CI until an entry or an exclusion covers it. The upstream watch turns each release into a pull request with the coverage change. After v1.0, XDRProof is maintained for 18 months: new protocol shapes, issue triage, and a release for each protocol upgrade."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-57","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#independence","content":"The corpus, schema, tools and adapter contract are Apache-2.0 and run from a clone, with no XDRProof service in the path, so anyone can keep using them whatever happens to the maintainer. GitHub hosts the source, CI and releases; this site is generated from the repository. The tools collect no analytics. Milestone status and protocol-upgrade notes are posted publicly in the repository."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-58","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#security","content":"The generator runs stellar-core with no network, no keys and no funds, and nothing in the repository holds a secret. Every generated ledger is reproducible from its recipe. The replay harness runs each indexer in a container with a memory limit, and treats an out-of-memory kill as a crash."},"2171":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-59","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice                                                               | Why                          |\n| ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Ledgers | stellar-core v30.0.0 (Docker, pinned digest), SDF's public data lake | the bytes the network writes |","table":"table-2"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-60","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part               | Choice                                                  | Why                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Reference decoding | rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0 (native), stellar-core `dump-xdr` | independent of every SDK under test |","table":"table-2"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-61","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice        | Why                                    |\n| ------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| Tooling | Node.js, Rust | the corpus tools and the native reader |","table":"table-2"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-62","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part   | Choice                   | Why                                                                   |\n| ------ | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Linter | Go, `golang.org/x/tools` | the language of wallet-backend, stellar-etl and the SDK's ingest code |","table":"table-2"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-63","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part   | Choice                                                  | Why                                  |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| Replay | Docker, TimescaleDB (wallet-backend's own pinned image) | each indexer in its real environment |","table":"table-2"},"2176":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-64","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice     | Why |\n| ------- | ---------- | --- |\n| Licence | Apache-2.0 |     |","table":"table-2"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-65","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"The same document, with more detail, is `ARCHITECTURE.md` in the repository."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","type":"page","content":"Concepts","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/concepts"},"2179":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-0","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"The handful of words the corpus uses: entry, variant, hazard, placement, fact, evidence, focus, change order."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-1","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"**What it is.** The few words these pages use, each explained in one line."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-2","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"**Why it matters.** The corpus is exact about what an entry, a variant or a fact is. Knowing these words makes every other page quicker to read."},"2182":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-3","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Entry** | One rare shape. A directory under `entries/` with an `entry.json` and one XDR file per variant, plus up to two alternate-ordering files when the change order varies. Its `id` is permanent and never reused. |","table":"table-0"},"2183":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-4","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Catalog file** | The hand-written source of an entry, `catalog/<id>.json`: its intent and facts, addressed relative to the focus transaction. `make build` (`tools/build.mjs`) compiles it into the entry. |","table":"table-0"},"2184":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-5","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                 |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Family** | The first part of an entry id, such as `failure`, `signer` or `archival`. The docs group entries by it. |","table":"table-0"},"2185":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-6","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Variant** | One ledger file of an entry, for one protocol, one network passphrase and one emitter-flag profile. Its key reads like `p30-mainnet-generated`; a `-galexie` or `-bare` suffix names a non-base profile. |","table":"table-0"},"2186":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-7","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Flag profile** | Which of stellar-core's six metadata flags a run sets: `base` (the shipped variants), `galexie` (all six, as public data lakes are written) or `bare` (none). The ledger is the same; the metadata is not. |","table":"table-0"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-8","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Lane** | How a variant was made: `generated` by a standalone stellar-core, `mined` from ledger history, or `synthetic` (hand-built; none yet). The recipes are also split into two lanes, classic and Soroban. |","table":"table-0"},"2188":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-9","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Hazard** | A named way readers break, with its evidence, from `caps/registry.json`. For example, `H-A3` is a signed-payload signer stored as 33 bytes. |","table":"table-0"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-10","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Placement** | Where in the XDR a hazard sits in this entry. For example, `SetOptionsOp.signer.key`. |","table":"table-0"},"2190":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-11","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                 |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Fact** | A value a correct reader must find at a JSON Pointer path in the SEP-0051 rendering, as `equals`, `length` or `some` (a matching change, in any order). |","table":"table-0"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-12","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word         | Meaning                                                                      |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Evidence** | What backs a fact: `construction`, `snapshot`, `core`, `walker` or `review`. |","table":"table-0"},"2192":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-13","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **SDK check** | A rule for an SDK helper rather than a value, such as \"return all 5 entries or raise an error\". |","table":"table-0"},"2193":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-14","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                               |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Focus** | The transaction (and operation) the entry is about, so a reader knows where to look in a full ledger. |","table":"table-0"},"2194":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-15","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Change order** | Whether stellar-core always emits the operation's changes in the same order. When it doesn't, the entry pins an order-normalised hash and lists every ordering seen. |","table":"table-0"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-16","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Channel** | `stable`, `next` (gated CAPs before mainnet) or `embargoed` (never committed publicly). All entries today are `stable`. |","table":"table-0"},"2196":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"page","content":"How it works","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works"},"2197":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-0","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"Recipes drive a bare stellar-core to emit rare shapes; the bytes become entries with facts; independent readers check them, real indexers ingest them, and SDK builds read the same bytes."},"2198":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-1","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"1. Make the shapes"},"2199":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-2","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"2. Keep the bytes, write down the truth"},"2200":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-3","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"3. Check the truth independently"},"2201":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-4","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"4. Run it against real code"},"2202":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-5","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"**What it is.** The whole pipeline in four steps: make rare ledgers, write down what a correct reader must find, check that with independent readers, then run real indexers, SDKs and indexer code against it."},"2203":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-6","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"**Why it matters.** Each step is one `make` command, so any claim on this site can be re-checked."},"2204":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-7","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"The full map, with every tool and its numbers:"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-8","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"A **recipe** is a deterministic script that drives one fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 over its HTTP port. The core image is pinned by digest. There is no RPC, no network, and no keys or funds: seeds and close times are fixed. Core writes every closed ledger as `LedgerCloseMeta`."},"2206":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-9","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"Some shapes only happen on a real network, or are worth having as real network bytes, so they are **mined** from public ledger history instead. `mined/` holds  real mainnet and testnet ledgers;  entries are built from  of them, the testnet ledger where wallet-backend halted among them."},"2207":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-10","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"Each interesting ledger becomes a corpus **entry**, compiled from a hand-written catalog file. An entry holds:"},"2208":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-11","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the raw bytes;"},"2209":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-12","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the CAP and the hazard it targets;"},"2210":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-13","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"where in the XDR the hazard sits;"},"2211":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-14","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the **facts** a correct reader must find, each with what backs it."},"2212":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-15","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                | Reader                                                                         | Target        |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| Byte-for-byte round trip, every fact | native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0; the WASM build must agree wherever it can decode | `make verify` |","table":"table-0"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-16","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                    | Reader                                                           | Target        |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| Fact values also read by | stellar-core's own `dump-xdr` (C++) and XDRProof's schema walker | `make oracle` |","table":"table-0"},"2214":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-17","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                        | Reader                                         | Target          |\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| Which union arms are reached | a Python walker over the v30.0 XDR definitions | `make coverage` |","table":"table-0"},"2215":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-18","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                                                                  | Reader                             | Target                 |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| Every arm reached by an entry, or classified with the core source line that decides it | the schema walker, per transaction | `make coverage-report` |","table":"table-0"},"2216":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-19","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                 | Reader                                                                  | Target         |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| No entry is redundant | leave-one-out over each entry's arms, hazards, sightings and placements | `make novelty` |","table":"table-0"},"2217":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-20","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against  | How                                                                                                                                                                             | Target             |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| The SDKs | builds of the JavaScript, WASM, Rust, Go and Python SDKs read every corpus variant, plus strkey, invalid-input and depth vectors;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions | `make conformance` |","table":"table-1"},"2218":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-21","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against    | How                                                                                                          | Target        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| An indexer | real wallet-backend builds ingest every corpus ledger, one isolated session each, through a mock stellar-rpc | `make replay` |","table":"table-1"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-22","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against             | How                                                                                                       | Target      |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Indexer source code | `xplint` follows each value from where it came from to where it is kept, for the three ingestion mistakes | `make lint` |","table":"table-1"},"2220":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-23","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"The figure marks two pieces as planned: a GitHub Action and the hardening guide. Today every check runs as a `make` target. Architecture says what is built and what the grant funds."},"2221":{"id":"/docs/start","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"page","content":"What XDRProof is","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start"},"2222":{"id":"/docs/start-0","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"A shared corpus of rare but valid Stellar ledger data, the facts a correct reader must find in it, and tools that replay it into indexers and test SDKs on the same shapes."},"2223":{"id":"/docs/start-1","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#one-ledger-start-to-finish","content":"One ledger, start to finish"},"2224":{"id":"/docs/start-2","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"What is in it"},"2225":{"id":"/docs/start-3","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"Check every claim yourself"},"2226":{"id":"/docs/start-4","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"What it has found"},"2227":{"id":"/docs/start-5","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**What it is.** A free, open test kit for any program that reads the Stellar ledger: indexers, wallets, explorers and SDKs. It holds real ledger data in its rarest valid shapes, the answers a correct program must give, and tools that check your code against them."},"2228":{"id":"/docs/start-6","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**Why it matters.** Most ledgers are ordinary, so the rare shapes go untested until one arrives in production, where it can stop an indexer or store a wrong balance. XDRProof lets every team meet them in a test first."},"2229":{"id":"/docs/start-7","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"An indexer has run fine for months. Then a ledger arrives with a shape its code has never seen:"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/start-8","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a signer that carries a payload;"},"2231":{"id":"/docs/start-9","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a trustline created by a contract call, with no event;"},"2232":{"id":"/docs/start-10","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a map whose keys all read as \"1\"."},"2233":{"id":"/docs/start-11","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"Ingestion halts, or worse, it keeps going and stores something wrong. The team fixes it. Next month another team, in another language, meets the same shape."},"2234":{"id":"/docs/start-12","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**XDRProof turns each of those shapes into a test everyone can run.** One team's incident becomes every team's regression test, and one SDK's fix becomes a check on all of them."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/start-13","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part       | What it is                                                                                                     | Where      |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Corpus** | entries of real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes, each tagged with its CAP, with the facts a correct reader must return | `entries/` |","table":"table-0"},"2236":{"id":"/docs/start-14","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                | What it is                                                   | Where                |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- |\n| **Hazard registry** | named ways readers break, in  layers, each with its evidence | `caps/registry.json` |","table":"table-0"},"2237":{"id":"/docs/start-15","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                    | What it is                                                           | Where       |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| **Schema and manifest** | JSON Schema 2020-12 for an entry; a generated index of every variant | `schema/v1` |","table":"table-0"},"2238":{"id":"/docs/start-16","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part              | What it is                                                                                                                                 | Where    |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------- |\n| **Mined ledgers** | real mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, for shapes only a real network produces, or worth having as real network bytes | `mined/` |","table":"table-0"},"2239":{"id":"/docs/start-17","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part        | What it is                                                                              | Where      |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Recipes** | Transactions for a bare standalone stellar-core that regenerate every generated variant | `recipes/` |","table":"table-0"},"2240":{"id":"/docs/start-18","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part      | What it is                                                                                   | Where    |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| **Tools** | Verify, oracle, coverage, generate, order scan, conformance and replay, each a `make` target | `tools/` |","table":"table-0"},"2241":{"id":"/docs/start-19","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part       | What it is                                                                                                                                                           | Where     |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| **Linter** | `xplint`, which points at the lines of Go indexer code that make the three ingestion mistakes:  real findings on wallet-backend `main`,  with XDRProof's two patches | `linter/` |","table":"table-0"},"2242":{"id":"/docs/start-20","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"The numbers on these pages come from the repository's own data or from a `make` target you can run, and each page names which. Start with Quickstart."},"2243":{"id":"/docs/start-21","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"Its runs found two bugs nobody had reported. Both are filed upstream with a figure and a reproduction: wallet-backend#834 and js-stellar-xdr-json#67. For an issue that was already open, wallet-backend#820, XDRProof offered a tested fix. See Reported upstream."},"2244":{"id":"/docs/start-22","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"The RFP also asks for an **indexer-hardening guide**, and XDRProof will ship as a **GitHub Action** and packages for JavaScript, Go and Rust. These are what the grant funds; see Architecture. The registry already marks the hazards the guide will cover."},"2245":{"id":"/docs/start-23","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"This repository backs a proposal to the Stellar Community Fund's RFP *XDR/CAP-Coverage Audit Tool and Indexer-Hardening Guide* (SCF #46). The corpus grows entry by entry from here. XDRProof is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by the Stellar Development Foundation."},"2246":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart"},"2247":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-0","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"Clone the repository and run make verify: strkey vectors, every entry against its schema, hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip and every fact, and every mined ledger, in seconds."},"2248":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-1","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#get-the-code","content":"Get the code"},"2249":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-2","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"Check what you have"},"2250":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-3","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"Verify the corpus"},"2251":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-4","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Go further"},"2252":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-5","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"**What it is.** The shortest path from nothing to a checked corpus: clone the repository, run one command, read the result."},"2253":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-6","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"**Why it matters.** You don't have to trust any number on this site. `make verify` re-checks every entry on your own machine in seconds."},"2254":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-7","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `make verify` | Node ≥ 22.15 and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2255":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-8","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target          | Needs                                                                         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage` | Python 3 and Node ≥ 22.15 (`coverage-goldens` also needs git and the network) |","table":"table-0"},"2256":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-9","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                               | Needs                              |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage-report`, `make novelty`, `make build` | Node ≥ 22.15, npm and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2257":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-10","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                     |\n| ------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `make oracle` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm |","table":"table-0"},"2258":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-11","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                                    | Needs                                     |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `make generate`, `make generate-quick`, `make order-scan` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq, perl |","table":"table-0"},"2259":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-12","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target             | Needs              |\n| ------------------ | ------------------ |\n| `make conformance` | Node, Rust, Docker |","table":"table-0"},"2260":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-13","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                                                              |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `make replay` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git; the network on the first run only |","table":"table-0"},"2261":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-14","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                            | Needs                                                             |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `make lint-selftest`, `make lint` | Docker (a pinned Go image); `make lint` also Node ≥ 22.15 and git |","table":"table-0"},"2262":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-15","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"The first run installs the tool dependencies (`npm ci`) and builds the native reader (`cargo build --release`), about a minute. Then it checks:"},"2263":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-16","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every SEP-0023 strkey test vector;"},"2264":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-17","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every entry against the schema, and its hazards against the registry;"},"2265":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-18","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"size and sha256;"},"2266":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-19","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"a decode and re-encode through **native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0** that must reproduce identical bytes;"},"2267":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-20","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the focus transaction is in the ledger;"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-21","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every expected fact;"},"2269":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-22","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json`, wherever it can decode, renders the same JSON;"},"2270":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-23","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that `manifest.json` is current;"},"2271":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-24","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every mined ledger and its findings."},"2272":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-25","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Each target has its own page under Tools. `make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."},"2273":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"page","content":"Conformance","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance"},"2274":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"make conformance: every corpus variant, the strkey vectors, invalid XDR and a depth ladder through builds of the JavaScript, Go, Rust and Python SDKs. Each build's known failures are written down first; anything else fails the run."},"2275":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"The builds"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"What each family checks"},"2277":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"The recorded matrix"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Every known failure, and why"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"How deep each SDK reads"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"**What it is.** The same corpus bytes and test vectors, run through  builds of the JavaScript, WebAssembly, Rust, Go and Python SDKs, with every answer compared;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions."},"2281":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"**Why it matters.** When two SDKs read the same bytes differently, one of them is wrong, and every app built on it inherits the mistake. This finds it first."},"2282":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"Needs Node ≥ 22.15, Rust and Docker. Every SDK build answers the same questions about the same bytes, and the run prints one matrix: which SDK reads which ledger correctly."},"2283":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Each build runs as a **testee**: a small process that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and answers one JSON line on stdout. The runner (`conformance/run.mjs`) judges every answer against a reference and against that build's pre-registered expectations."},"2284":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee      | Package                                                                                     | Families |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `js@17.4.0` | `@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0 (its XDR layer is `@stellar/js-xdr` 5, written in JavaScript) | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2285":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee            | Package                                                             | Families              |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `xdr-json@30.0.0` | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM | corpus, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2286":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee        | Package                                          | Families                      |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `rust@30.0.0` | `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 with `stellar-strkey` 1.0.0 | all but the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2287":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee      | Package                                    | Families                                        |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `go@v0.9.0` | `github.com/stellar/go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0 | all but SEP-0051 JSON and the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2288":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee          | Package                                                     | Families |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `python@17.0.1` | `stellar-sdk` 17.0.1 (py-stellar-base), the current release | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2289":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee             | Package                                                                                                | Families                      |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `python-xdr@0.2.1` | `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 and `stellar-strkey` 0.2.0, which py-stellar-base's next major release is built on | corpus, strkey, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2290":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee                                                              | Package                                      | Families  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `js@17.2.0` · `js@17.2.1` · `js@17.3.0` · `go@v0.7.1` · `go@v0.7.2` | the releases before and after upstream fixes | sightings |","table":"table-0"},"2291":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Java, Swift, Dart, PHP and .NET SDKs can join by implementing the same protocol: `hello`, `decode`, `encode_json`, `strkey_decode` and `native`. A testee answers `unsupported` for anything its SDK cannot do, and that is recorded as not applicable, not as a failure."},"2292":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                        | Passes when                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `corpus` | every variant of every entry | it decodes; the re-encoding is byte-identical; where the SDK renders SEP-0051 JSON, the JSON equals rs-stellar-xdr's and every expected fact of the entry holds on it |","table":"table-1"},"2293":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                         | Passes when                                                                |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `strkey` | the SEP-0023 test vectors, plus a deterministic sample of every kind of address in the corpus | valid keys decode to the right kind and payload; invalid keys are rejected |","table":"table-1"},"2294":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Passes when                                                      |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `lane-s` | hand-built invalid XDR: truncated, trailing bytes, a bool of 2, unknown and negative discriminants, a symbol past its maximum, non-zero padding, an optional flag of 2, a huge length with no data, a path longer than five | every invalid input is rejected; the valid controls are accepted |","table":"table-1"},"2295":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family  | Input                                     | Passes when                                                                |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `depth` | an `ScVal` vector nested 64 to 4,000 deep | measured: the deepest value each SDK decodes, and whether anything crashes |","table":"table-1"},"2296":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                              | Passes when                                                                                           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sdk-check` | each entry's `expected.sdk_checks` | the helper behaves as the check says, for example a native projection keeps every map entry or raises |","table":"table-1"},"2297":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                                | Passes when                                                            |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sightings` | `conformance/vectors/sightings.json` | the upstream fix behaves as fixed; the releases before it show the bug |","table":"table-1"},"2298":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"This is `conformance/recorded/matrix.md` from the last run. **known** means a failure that is written down in that build's expectations, with its cause."},"2299":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"`js@17.4.0` and `python-xdr@0.2.1` read every corpus variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does: the bytes, the SEP-0051 JSON and every expected fact."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Each build's `conformance/expectations/<testee>.json` lists every check it is known to fail, with the reason and the finding it belongs to. They are written from a measured run and reviewed by hand. After that the run must match them exactly: **an unexpected failure and an unexpected pass both fail it**, so a new bug and an upstream fix both show up."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"Nothing crashes on the depth ladder; each SDK refuses cleanly past its limit. The deepest sampled depth each one decodes:"},"2302":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"A depth limit counts XDR nesting, and one `ScVal` level costs several units, so equal limits don't mean equal reach: the WASM decoder and the Python packages both use 500 and differ fourfold. The corpus has ledgers stellar-core applied whose Soroban authorization tree is 245 levels deep, and the WASM decoder refuses them (finding F-1 on Findings)."},"2303":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"page","content":"Coverage","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage"},"2304":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"make coverage and make coverage-report: every XDR union arm reachable from LedgerCloseMeta is either reached by a corpus entry or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it."},"2305":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Every arm accounted for"},"2306":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"**What it is.** A count of every kind of data a ledger can hold (every branch of every XDR union), and whether the corpus has a real example of each."},"2307":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"**Why it matters.** It turns \"we test the rare cases\" into a number. Every branch the corpus lacks has a written reason, with the stellar-core source line behind it."},"2308":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"A walker reads the rs-stellar-xdr definitions for v30.0, and lists every union arm and enum member reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta`. It then walks real ledgers and records which arms they meet."},"2309":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                          | Union arms               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` | **** (and  enum members) |","table":"table-0"},"2310":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                                                           | Union arms |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entry files ( variant files: raw `.xdr`, alternate change orderings, and `.xdr.zst` decompressed) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2311":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                    | Union arms |\n| -------------------------- | ---------- |\n| The mined ledgers ( files) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2312":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                            | Union arms |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entries and mined ledgers together | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2313":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                               | Union arms |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| stellar-core's goldens (866 files, 15,306 ledgers, commit `f2b91947`) | 254 of 499 |","table":"table-0"},"2314":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"The corpus rows are computed from the corpus at commit , the same way `make coverage` computes them. The goldens row is a fixed measurement at stellar-core commit `f2b91947`. The goldens are the ledgers stellar-core's own tests produce."},"2315":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"`make coverage` counts every arm in an entry's file, including arms from other transactions that happen to share its ledger. `make coverage-report` is stricter. An entry **reaches** an arm only when its own transaction (envelope, fee changes, apply meta) or its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys) contains it. Every arm no entry reaches is classified in `coverage/exclusions.json`, with the stellar-core source line that decides it. The run fails if an arm is in neither list, or in both."},"2316":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Enum members reached by an entry:  of . The report is written to `COVERAGE.md`, with the entries that target each hazard."},"2317":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"No `todo` arm is left: every arm stellar-core can still write at protocol 30 on a standalone network is in an entry. Only the  `network-only` arms, which need a live network, are out of reach. A closed protocol-30 ledger never carries a `validation-only` or `not-emitted` arm, and a `historical` one appears only in ledgers of older protocols."},"2318":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"page","content":"Generate","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate"},"2319":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"make generate: 20 fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 runs, in pairs, over both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair must agree, and every shipped generated variant must come out again."},"2320":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"The matrix"},"2321":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#what-must-hold","content":"What must hold"},"2322":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"Each pair agrees"},"2323":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"Every shipped variant is reproduced"},"2324":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The profiles are compared"},"2325":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"**What it is.** Rebuilds every generated ledger from scratch on fresh, offline stellar-cores, twice, and checks that the runs agree."},"2326":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"**Why it matters.** It proves the corpus is reproducible: nothing in it depends on our machine, our timing or our luck."},"2327":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"It needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq and perl, and host ports 21700–21719. The stellar-core image is pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and every run refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0."},"2328":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"`make generate` runs **10 pairs**, which is 20 fresh standalone cores, 6 at a time. There is no RPC, no network, and no keys or funds."},"2329":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2330":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2331":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2332":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P28      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2333":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2334":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"The two runs of a pair are compared ledger by ledger. They must be byte-identical, or identical after normalising. Normalising sorts the order of changes within an operation and zeroes the diagnostic `invoke_time_nsecs` timing value."},"2335":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"The list of shipped generated variants is read from `entries/` (`tools/shipped-checks.mjs`), so the testnet and protocol-28 variants are re-checked too. A full run also fails if any shipped variant was not assigned to a pair."},"2336":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The same recipe ledgers under the three flag profiles are compared, and the result is recorded in `recipes/<lane>/profiles/p30-mainnet.json`. Every ledger keeps the same header hash; only the metadata changes. See Found while building."},"2337":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"It ends with `OK: every run reproduced; every shipped generated variant reproduced`."},"2338":{"id":"/docs/tools","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"page","content":"Tools","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools"},"2339":{"id":"/docs/tools-0","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"Every check is a make target: verify, oracle, coverage, novelty, generate, order scan, conformance and replay. Each one re-runs a claim and prints its result."},"2340":{"id":"/docs/tools-1","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"The readers behind them"},"2341":{"id":"/docs/tools-2","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"**What it is.** Every check XDRProof runs, each one a `make` target."},"2342":{"id":"/docs/tools-3","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"**Why it matters.** Every number on this site comes from one of these commands, and each prints its result on your machine."},"2343":{"id":"/docs/tools-4","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Needs              | Time    |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make verify` | Strkey vectors hold. Every entry matches the schema, sizes and hashes match, and every variant round-trips byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0. The focus transaction is present, every fact holds, the WASM reader renders the same JSON wherever it can decode, and the manifest is current. Every mined ledger is verified | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2344":{"id":"/docs/tools-5","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                          | Needs                     | Time    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------- |\n| `make oracle` | Each fact's values are also read by stellar-core's own decoder and by the schema walker | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2345":{"id":"/docs/tools-6","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                  | Needs                  | Time    |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------- |\n| `make coverage` | union arms are reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` at v30.0; the entries reach , the mined ledgers | Python 3, Node ≥ 22.15 | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2346":{"id":"/docs/tools-7","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                 | What it proves                                                                                                                                                              | Needs              | Time    |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make coverage-report` | Every one of the  arms is reached by an entry's own transaction or ledger shape () or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it (). Writes `COVERAGE.md` | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2347":{"id":"/docs/tools-8","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target         | What it proves                                                                                        | Needs              | Time    |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make novelty` | No entry is redundant: leave-one-out over  atoms (arms, enum members, hazards, sightings, placements) | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2348":{"id":"/docs/tools-9","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                  | What it proves                                                  | Needs                  | Time        |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------- |\n| `make coverage-goldens` | stellar-core's own 866 golden ledger files reach 254 of the 499 | Python 3, git, network | about 1 min |","table":"table-0"},"2349":{"id":"/docs/tools-10","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                      | Needs                             | Time         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| `make generate` | 20 fresh standalone cores, in pairs, across both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair agrees, and every shipped generated variant is reproduced | Docker, Node, npm, curl, jq, perl | about 10 min |","table":"table-0"},"2350":{"id":"/docs/tools-11","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                | What it proves                      | Needs    | Time        |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------- | ----------- |\n| `make generate-quick` | The two mainnet base P30 pairs only | the same | about 3 min |","table":"table-0"},"2351":{"id":"/docs/tools-12","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target            | What it proves                                                              | Needs  | Time         |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ------------ |\n| `make order-scan` | fresh runs per lane: which ledgers vary in change order; content never does | Docker | about 15 min |","table":"table-0"},"2352":{"id":"/docs/tools-13","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target             | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Needs              | Time             |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------- |\n| `make conformance` | Every corpus variant, the strkeys, the invalid-input vectors and a depth ladder go through  SDK builds;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0 and `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 read every variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does; every other difference is pinned with its cause | Node, Rust, Docker | about 2 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2353":{"id":"/docs/tools-14","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                 | Needs                           | Time              |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `make replay` | The whole corpus into wallet-backend `main` ( of  handled,  halted), and every fixed failure before and after its fix. Every verdict is pinned | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git | about 20 min cold |","table":"table-0"},"2354":{"id":"/docs/tools-15","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target               | What it proves                                                                            | Needs  | Time             |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ---------------- |\n| `make lint-selftest` | The linter catches all  seeded violations and all  mutants, and flags none of  good cases | Docker | about 2 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2355":{"id":"/docs/tools-16","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target      | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Needs                     | Time             |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| `make lint` | The linter on real code: wallet-backend `main` ( findings, all true;  with the two patches), stellar-etl (), go-stellar-sdk ingest (), the commits around upstream fixes and fixed bugs put back. Every finding is pinned and triaged | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, git | about 3 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2356":{"id":"/docs/tools-17","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."},"2357":{"id":"/docs/tools-18","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make build` is not a check: it compiles `catalog/` into `entries/` from the runs of `make generate` and `mined/`, in minutes. See Catalog."},"2358":{"id":"/docs/tools-19","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Native reader | A small Rust program on `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 (depth limit 1500) that decodes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and re-encodes | verify, novelty, coverage-report, conformance (the reference), replay |","table":"table-1"},"2359":{"id":"/docs/tools-20","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader      | What it is                                           | Used by             |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| WASM reader | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, a second opinion | verify, conformance |","table":"table-1"},"2360":{"id":"/docs/tools-21","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader         | What it is                                                                | Used by |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `stellar-core` | Core's own C++ `dump-xdr`, in Docker with no network, on the pinned image | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2361":{"id":"/docs/tools-22","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- |\n| Schema walker | `walker.mjs`: XDRProof's own JavaScript reader, driven only by the XDR definitions; it shares no code with any SDK | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2362":{"id":"/docs/tools-23","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader          | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by  |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------- |\n| Coverage walker | `xdrcov.py`: a Python walker over the same v30.0 definitions that records every union arm and enum member it meets | coverage |","table":"table-1"},"2363":{"id":"/docs/tools-24","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader | What it is                                                                       | Used by             |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| xplint | Go analyzers over SSA (`golang.org/x/tools`), built and run in a pinned Go image | lint, lint-selftest |","table":"table-1"},"2364":{"id":"/docs/tools-25","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader         | What it is                                                                                                                             | Used by                  |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Segment walker | the schema walker again, attributing every arm it meets to the transaction envelope or result meta it sits in, or to the ledger itself | coverage-report, novelty |","table":"table-1"},"2365":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"page","content":"Linter","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter"},"2366":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"make lint and make lint-selftest: xplint reads Go indexer code and points at the lines that make the three ingestion mistakes the RFP names. Text, JSON or SARIF for GitHub code scanning. Every finding on real code is triaged and pinned."},"2367":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"Run it"},"2368":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#the-three-mistakes","content":"The three mistakes"},"2369":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"How it decides"},"2370":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#on-real-code","content":"On real code"},"2371":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#wallet-backend-main","content":"wallet-backend `main`"},"2372":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#stellar-etl-master","content":"stellar-etl `master`"},"2373":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#before-and-after-each-upstream-fix","content":"Before and after each upstream fix"},"2374":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#fixed-bugs-put-back","content":"Fixed bugs put back"},"2375":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"Self-test"},"2376":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Limits"},"2377":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"**What it is.** `xplint` reads the Go source of an indexer and points at the lines that make one of three mistakes: trusting a field stellar-core never checked, walking a nested structure with no limit, or taking a transaction's participants from what it asked for instead of what happened."},"2378":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"**Why it matters.** These three mistakes are how real indexers have halted or stored wrong data. The linter finds the code patterns themselves, whether or not a ledger has triggered them yet. On wallet-backend `main` it reports , all real code patterns and none reachable by a ledger today: one is hardening at today's limits, three sit in code Blend's contracts cannot reach. With XDRProof's two patches it reports ."},"2379":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"The exit status is 0 with no findings, 1 with findings, and 2 when the packages could not be loaded. It needs the same Go toolchain and cgo setup the packages build with."},"2380":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"In this repository, both targets run in a pinned Go image (`linter/toolchain-image`) through `linter/go.sh`:"},"2381":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#the-three-mistakes","content":"rules,  sub-rules on by default ( in all). Each sub-rule has a stable ID, used as the SARIF `ruleId`. The table is read from `linter/xplint/rules.go`."},"2382":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Where a value came from.** Each value carries labels for its source: text bytes, a possibly muxed address, a number a contract chose, the declared footprint, the authorization tree, an operation body, a diagnostic event. Sources are named SDK calls, conversions and field reads."},"2383":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Where it is kept.** A sink is where a value is stored: a JSON- or db-tagged field, a map key, a set member, a builder's account. A map or set counts only when it outlives the function; a scratch map for de-duplication is not attribution."},"2384":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**What clears it.** A check counts only when every path to the sink passes through it, and only for the value it checked: checking `good` does not clear `bad + good`. A success check clears operation bodies and events on its branch."},"2385":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Recursion.** XP002 finds recursive walks over the XDR types that can nest, computed from the SDK's own type graph, so CAP-0071's nested delegates are covered without a list. A budget must stop the walk; a budget that only logs, or that fails the ledger, is reported."},"2386":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#on-real-code","content":"Not yet on unmodified `main`: its  findings are real. With XDRProof's two tested patches, offered upstream, xplint reports . Until an unmodified current revision runs clean, this check stays open."},"2387":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#wallet-backend-main","content":"The unbounded walk is wallet-backend#820: today's deepest tree (245 levels) survives it, so it is hardening. XDRProof's patch bounds it at depth 256 and 4,096 nodes and stops, counted and logged, instead of failing; it is offered on #820. The three Blend sites are the shape wallet-backend fixed in its SEP-41 processor (#756), not reachable today. Both patches are in `linter/patches/`; with them, wallet-backend's own build, vet and tests pass."},"2388":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#before-and-after-each-upstream-fix","content":"The same rules on the wallet-backend commits around fixes of these mistakes. Each fixed bug is caught before its fix and gone after it."},"2389":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#fixed-bugs-put-back","content":"Mutants of current wallet-backend `main`: a fixed bug put back, or a guard removed. Each must make its rule fire."},"2390":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"`make lint-selftest` runs xplint on seeded fixtures, each modelled on real code and named in its fixture, then mutates the good cases."},"2391":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"The near misses are seeded too: a check on a different memo than the one stored, a bound on the wrong branch, `max` used as a bound, a helper that calls the UTF-8 check and ignores it, a budget that only logs, a method that happens to be called `Successful`."},"2392":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Calls through interfaces and function values are not followed (closures are), nor values sent over channels."},"2393":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Flows through a database are not followed: wallet-backend #802 (Blend decimals stored, read back and used as an exponent) is out of reach; the in-memory shape is caught."},"2394":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Memory is tracked one struct field deep; deeper fields and slice elements share their root's labels."},"2395":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-29","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"The sources and sinks are a catalogue of go-stellar-sdk and standard-library calls (`xplint/sdk.go`); code that reads XDR through its own types needs entries added."},"2396":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-30","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"No false-positive rate is claimed. Every finding on the three codebases and on the history commits is triaged true, but three codebases are too few to state a rate."},"2397":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-31","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"The full list is in `linter/README.md`."},"2398":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"page","content":"Novelty","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty"},"2399":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"make novelty: leave-one-out over every entry's arms, enum members, hazards, sightings and placements. The run fails if any entry holds nothing another entry doesn't."},"2400":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"Today"},"2401":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"**What it is.** A check that every entry earns its place: each must hold something no other entry holds."},"2402":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"**Why it matters.** The corpus grows by purpose, not by volume, so a team can run all of it without wading through duplicates."},"2403":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"The corpus grows by purpose, not volume. Novelty checks that every entry earns its place. It describes each entry as a set of **atoms**:"},"2404":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom                        | What counts                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Union arms and enum members | those reached by the entry's own transaction (its envelope and result meta) or by its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys), in any variant. Other transactions that share the ledger count for nothing |","table":"table-0"},"2405":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom    | What counts                   |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Hazards | each hazard the entry targets |","table":"table-0"},"2406":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom      | What counts                       |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Sightings | each upstream issue it reproduces |","table":"table-0"},"2407":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom       | What counts                                        |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Placements | each pair of a hazard and where in the XDR it sits |","table":"table-0"},"2408":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"An entry is **redundant** when every one of its atoms is also held by some other entry. Each entry is left out in turn, so the result does not depend on the order entries are read, unlike a greedy \"does it add anything new\" pass. The run exits 1 if any entry is redundant."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"At commit :  redundant entries, over  atoms."},"2410":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                           | Count |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| Hold an arm, enum member, hazard or sighting no other entry holds |       |","table":"table-1"},"2411":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                        | Count |\n| ------------------------------ | ----- |\n| Told apart only by a placement |       |","table":"table-1"},"2412":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                        | Count |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| … of which every other atom sits inside one single other entry |       |","table":"table-1"},"2413":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"<Callout title=\"Placement is a claim, not a measurement\">A placement is text the author wrote. So `make novelty` lists every entry told apart only by placement, and flags the ones wholly contained in one other entry: for each such pair, the facts must check a different thing.</Callout>"},"2414":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"It needs Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (Rust), which finds each variant's transaction hashes."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"page","content":"Oracle","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle"},"2416":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"make oracle: each fact's values must also appear in what stellar-core's own C++ decoder reads and in what XDRProof's schema walker reads. No SDK is involved."},"2417":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A planted error must be caught"},"2418":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"**What it is.** A second and a third opinion on every expected value, from two readers that share no code with any SDK: stellar-core's own decoder and XDRProof's own walker."},"2419":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"**Why it matters.** A test is only as good as its expected answer. Readers that cannot share a bug with the SDK under test keep the answers honest."},"2420":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"A fact is only as good as the reader that produced it. The oracle asks two readers that share no code with any SDK:"},"2421":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader           | How it runs                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **stellar-core** | `stellar-core dump-xdr`, inside `docker run --network none` on the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image` (override with `XDRPROOF_CORE_IMAGE`) |","table":"table-0"},"2422":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader            | How it runs                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Schema walker** | `tools/walker.mjs`, driven only by the v30.0 XDR definitions. It rejects non-zero padding, over-length values, bad enums and trailing bytes |","table":"table-0"},"2423":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"For each fact, the oracle takes its expected values and checks that they appear among the values each reader produced. Enum values are checked too: `tools/coverage/defs/sep0051-enum-names.json` maps every XDR enum member to the string SEP-0051 prints for it, so the walker confirms a member it reached and stellar-core one its dump printed. Enum names are matched by name across all enums, anywhere in the ledger, so a common name such as `success` is a weak confirmation. A `length` fact has no value to check, and a `v0`-style arm name is structure, not a value. A value either reader misses is a **disagreement**, and the run fails. So does a ledger stellar-core cannot print: nothing in it is confirmed. With `--write`, it records `core` and `walker` in the fact's evidence."},"2424":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A reader that finds every value might simply accept anything. So the oracle tests itself. It takes every expected value of 10 or more digits (64-bit amounts, IDs and fees), changes its last digit and asks both readers again."},"2425":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                | Counts as |\n| ------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| Both readers reject the changed value | caught    |","table":"table-1"},"2426":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                                                      | Counts as                    |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Both readers find it: the changed value occurs elsewhere in the same ledger | a coincidence, not a failure |","table":"table-1"},"2427":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                   | Counts as                         |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------- |\n| Only one reader finds it | a **disagreement**: the run fails |","table":"table-1"},"2428":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"If no planted error is caught at all, the self-test fails and the run exits 1."},"2429":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"<Callout title=\"What it does not check\">It checks that a value is present in a reader's output, not that it sits at the fact's path; the path is checked by Verify.</Callout>"},"2430":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"page","content":"Order scan","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"make order-scan: 20 fresh runs per recipe lane record which ledgers come out in more than one byte order, and prove the content never varies."},"2432":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The committed results"},"2433":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"**What it is.** A measurement of how often stellar-core writes the same ledger's changes in a different order."},"2434":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"**Why it matters.** Code that assumes a fixed order works on one run and breaks on the next. The corpus ships the rarer orderings, so that code fails in a test instead."},"2435":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"stellar-core iterates a hash map with a per-process random salt, so the changes within an operation can come out in a different order on every run. The order scan measures this. It runs each recipe lane 20 times on fresh cores, records every byte ordering seen per ledger, and fails if any ledger's **content** differs once order is normalised."},"2436":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Results are in `recipes/classic/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json` and `recipes/soroban/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json`:"},"2437":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order               | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Classic |      | of  recipe ledgers;  differ in every run | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | -------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Soroban |      | of  recipe ledgers         | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2439":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The protocol-upgrade ledger (2) varies in both lanes as well; it is never an entry."},"2440":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Corpus entries whose order varies pin an order-normalised hash, and list every raw ordering seen (`change_order` in the schema)."},"2441":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"},"2442":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"page","content":"Replay","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay"},"2443":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"make replay: every corpus ledger into a real indexer through a mock stellar-rpc, one fresh store per ledger. Each variant gets a verdict from the indexer's own state: its cursor, its process and what it stored. Never from logs."},"2444":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"wallet-backend main, the whole corpus"},"2445":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Every fix, before and after"},"2446":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"A session"},"2447":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"Derive the probes"},"2448":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"Serve the ledger"},"2449":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"Prepare, start and watch"},"2450":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Observe"},"2451":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"Verdicts"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Probes"},"2453":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"Bring your own indexer"},"2454":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"**What it is.** Every corpus ledger fed to a real indexer, one at a time, through a mock of the RPC an indexer reads ledgers from, then a look at what the indexer stored."},"2455":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"**Why it matters.** An indexer that halts stops a wallet's history; one that stores a wrong balance is worse. Replay shows which ledgers do that before mainnet does."},"2456":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"Needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (`make native`). Once the images and Go modules are cached nothing reaches the network: the ledgers come from the corpus. On a laptop under Docker, six sessions at a time, the full run takes about 9.5 minutes once the builds are cached."},"2457":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"The reference indexer is stellar/wallet-backend. At `main` () it ingests  variants in  sessions: ** handled,  halted**. The other  are each pinned with their cause in `replay/expectations/wallet-backend@main.json`:"},"2458":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix (`replay/sightings.json`). Each must fail before and pass at the fix."},"2459":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"One session replays one ledger, one distinct variant (its bytes and its network), into a fresh store."},"2460":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"`replay/lib/derive.mjs` reads the ledger with rs-stellar-xdr and lists the **probes**: the facts a correct indexer has stored after ingesting it. They come from the ledger alone."},"2461":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"`replay/lib/mock-rpc.mjs` serves the ledger as stellar-rpc v30.0.1 would: `getLedgers`, `getHealth`, `getLatestLedger`, `getNetwork`, `getVersionInfo`, and `getLedgerEntries` from the state the ledger leaves behind. Pagination, page limits and validation errors follow stellar-rpc word for word."},"2462":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"The mock knows only what the ledger states, so it knows less than stellar-rpc in three ways, each recorded per session: a method it does not serve, or a request such as a history-archive fetch, is refused; a ledger entry the ledger does not touch is answered \"not found\", though on a real network it may exist; and a contract entry whose TTL the ledger leaves untouched is served without `liveUntilLedgerSeq`. A verdict other than `handled` in a session that read such state is flagged and traced in the indexer's code before it is recorded."},"2463":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"The adapter gives the indexer a clean store whose cursor sits just before the ledger and starts it against the mock. The harness polls the cursor until it passes the ledger, the process exits, the cursor stops moving, or the attempt times out. Once the ledger is passed, the process must still be running a second later. If the ledger was not passed, the indexer is started once more on the same store."},"2464":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"The adapter answers every probe from what the indexer stored, and the harness compares."},"2465":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Before any session counts, the harness replays the **controls**: the plainest ledger of each protocol the target can decode. Each must get past its ledger, or the run stops as a setup failure."},"2466":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `handled` | the cursor passed the ledger and every applicable probe matches |","table":"table-0"},"2467":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                          |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dropped` | the cursor passed, and a fact the ledger states is not stored |","table":"table-0"},"2468":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict      | Rule                                                                                 |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `mishandled` | the cursor passed, and a stored value differs from the ledger, or an invariant fails |","table":"table-0"},"2469":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                                                             |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `stalled` | the first attempt stopped making progress on the ledger without exiting; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2470":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `crashed` | the first attempt exited on the ledger; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2471":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict  | Rule                              |\n| -------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `halted` | neither attempt passed the ledger |","table":"table-0"},"2472":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-29","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict       | Rule                                                                                    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `unsupported` | the ledger's protocol is newer than the target decodes, or its network is not supported |","table":"table-0"},"2473":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-30","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict | Rule                                                         |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `error` | an adapter command failed; never a finding (the run exits 2) |","table":"table-0"},"2474":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-31","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Each probe names a fact by its key and states the expected value in a canonical form: strkeys, decimal strings, XDR enum names. A muxed `M…` account is named by its `G…` account."},"2475":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-32","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                                         | Expected                                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.present`, `tx.result`, `tx.fee_charged`, `tx.operations`, `tx.inner_hash` | the transaction is stored, with its result code, fee charged, operation count and, for a fee bump, the inner hash |","table":"table-1"},"2476":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-33","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                              | Expected                                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.participant`, `op.participant` | the source and fee source are attributed to the transaction; each operation's source to its operation |","table":"table-1"},"2477":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-34","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                    | Expected                                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `account.created`, `account.removed`, `contract.created` | the accounts and contract instances the transaction created or removed are recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2478":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-35","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                              | Expected                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `signer.added`, `signer.updated`, `signer.removed` | the signer change is recorded, with its new weight |","table":"table-1"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-36","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                  | Expected                                                     |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `trustline.added`, `trustline.removed` | the trustline the transaction created or removed is recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-37","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                 | Expected                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `balance.native`, `balance.trustline` | the balance at the end of the ledger, where the ledger changes it |","table":"table-1"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-38","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Two **invariants** cover everything the indexer stored in the session: `addresses.valid` (every stored address decodes and is one the ledger names) and `facts.extra` (every stored creation, merge and trustline change is one the ledger states). Facts on which indexers differ on purpose are not asserted."},"2482":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-39","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The indexer is reached only through an **adapter**: a directory under `replay/adapters/` with an `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands (`prepare`, `start` and `cursor` are required; `observe`, `setup`, `stop`, `finish` and `teardown` are optional). Any indexer that reads `getLedgers` can be tested the same way. An adapter that implements only the three required commands still gets `handled`, `crashed` and `halted` for every ledger. The full contract is in replay/README.md."},"2483":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-40","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The wallet-backend adapter builds each commit in a pinned Go image, runs TimescaleDB 2.28.2 as its compose file does, copies each session's database from a migrated template, runs `wallet-backend ingest` in live mode against the mock, and answers probes with one read of the session database."},"2484":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-41","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"<Callout title=\"A new failure, a changed one and a fixed one all fail the run\">`replay/expectations/<adapter>@<target>.json` lists every variant that is not `handled`, with the exact probes that failed, the cause and the upstream issue. It also pins the set of variants judged (their count and a sha256 of their ids), so none can drop out of a run unnoticed.</Callout>"},"2485":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"page","content":"Verify","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify"},"2486":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"make verify: strkey test vectors, then every entry against the schema, its hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact; then every mined ledger."},"2487":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#what-it-checks","content":"What it checks"},"2488":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Strkeys"},"2489":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry"},"2490":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"The bytes and the round trip"},"2491":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus and the facts"},"2492":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"A second reader"},"2493":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-manifest","content":"The manifest"},"2494":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-mined-ledgers","content":"The mined ledgers"},"2495":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-create-contract-authorization-payload","content":"The create-contract authorization payload"},"2496":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#output","content":"Output"},"2497":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"**What it is.** The basic check: every entry is exactly what it says it is, byte for byte, and every expected fact holds."},"2498":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"**Why it matters.** If the corpus were wrong, every test built on it would be wrong too. This runs in seconds, so it runs on every change."},"2499":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Every SEP-0023 test vector must decode and re-encode (valid), or be rejected (invalid). This covers XDRProof's own strkey codec, which the schema walker uses."},"2500":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry is validated against `schema/v1/entry.schema.json` (ajv, strict mode). Its `id` must match its directory, and every hazard id must exist in the registry. Variant keys must be unique, and each file name must start with its key."},"2501":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"Size and sha256 must match; `.zst` variants are checked on their decompressed bytes. The **native reader** (rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0, depth limit 1500) decodes the bytes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and encodes them again. The result must be identical, byte for byte."},"2502":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus transaction hash must be in the ledger, as an outer or fee-bump inner hash. Every fact that applies to the variant must hold at its JSON Pointer path. If the entry's change order is unstable, the order-normalised hash must match, and the shipped bytes must be one of the orderings seen."},"2503":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"The WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0 decodes each variant again, with no fallback. If it renders different JSON, the run fails. If it cannot decode a variant at all (for example at its depth limit), verify lists it and records `fails` in the manifest, and the run still passes. Today  of  variants agree."},"2504":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-manifest","content":"`manifest.json` is rebuilt from the entries. Without `--write`, verify fails if the committed file has drifted."},"2505":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-mined-ledgers","content":"`tools/mined-verify.mjs` checks every mined ledger. It must match its hash and size, round-trip through the native reader, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present."},"2506":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-create-contract-authorization-payload","content":"For the two v1 create-contract entries, `tools/auth-payload-check.mjs` encodes each authorization preimage by hand twice, once as the entry's own bytes and once as the V2 rendering the host hashes, and verifies the ed25519 signature against both. The entry stellar-core accepted verifies only as V2; the one signed over its own bytes verifies only as v1, and core failed it. No SDK or host code is involved. See hazard H-B16."},"2507":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#output","content":"A table with one row per variant: entry@variant, protocol, source, round trip, facts, WASM reader. Then a summary of the counts (entries, variants, hazards targeted), an `OK` line, and the mined-ledger report."}},"count":2507},"sorting":{"language":"multilingual","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{