tes are covered without a list. A budget must stop the walk; a budget that only logs, or that fails the ledger, is reported."],[2384,"**What clears it.** A check counts only when every path to the sink passes through it, and only for the value it checked: checking `good` does not clear `bad + good`. A success check clears operation bodies and events on its branch."],[2377,"**What it is.** `xplint` reads the Go source of an indexer and points at the lines that make one of three mistakes: trusting a field stellar-core never checked, walking a nested structure with no limit, or taking a transaction's participants from what it asked for instead of what happened."],[2401,"**What it is.** A check that every entry earns its place: each must hold something no other entry holds."],[2306,"**What it is.** A count of every kind of data a ledger can hold (every branch of every XDR union), and whether the corpus has a real example of each."],[2227,"**What it is.** A free, open test kit for any program that reads the Stellar ledger: indexers, wallets, explorers and SDKs. It holds real ledger data in its rarest valid shapes, the answers a correct program must give, and tools that check your code against them."],[2127,"**What it is.** A map of XDRProof: the corpus in the middle, the checks that prove it, and three tools that run it against real code: replay for indexers, conformance for SDKs, and a linter for indexer source."],[2433,"**What it is.** A measurement of how often stellar-core writes the same ledger's changes in a different order."],[2418,"**What it is.** A second and a third opinion on every expected value, from two readers that share no code with any SDK: stellar-core's own decoder and XDRProof's own walker."],[2341,"**What it is.** Every check XDRProof runs, each one a `make` target."],[2454,"**What it is.** Every corpus ledger fed to a real indexer, one at a time, through a mock of the RPC an indexer reads ledgers from, then a look at what the indexer stored."],[2057,"**What it is.** Every known way a program reading the Stellar ledger goes wrong, in  layers, each with its evidence."],[2038,"**What it is.** Real bugs that other teams found in wallet-backend and the SDKs, reproduced from corpus ledgers and test vectors."],[1924,"**What it is.** Real mainnet and testnet ledgers, downloaded from the public data lake with their hashes and the query that found them."],[2325,"**What it is.** Rebuilds every generated ledger from scratch on fresh, offline stellar-cores, twice, and checks that the runs agree."],[1943,"**What it is.** Small scripts that make a private, offline stellar-core produce each rare shape on demand."],[2497,"**What it is.** The basic check: every entry is exactly what it says it is, byte for byte, and every expected fact holds."],[2180,"**What it is.** The few words these pages use, each explained in one line."],[1970,"**What it is.** The format of an entry, field by field, as a JSON Schema."],[7,"**What it is.** The hand-written source of every entry: what it is for, where its ledger comes from, and the facts to check. A compiler turns it into the entry."],[1905,"**What it is.** The heart of XDRProof: real Stellar ledgers in their rarest valid shapes, each kept as the exact bytes stellar-core wrote, with the answers a correct reader must give."],[2280,"**What it is.** The same corpus bytes and test vectors, run through  builds of the JavaScript, WebAssembly, Rust, Go and Python SDKs, with every answer compared;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions."],[2252,"**What it is.** The shortest path from nothing to a checked corpus: clone the repository, run one command, read the result."],[2202,"**What it is.** The whole pipeline in four steps: make rare ledgers, write down what a correct reader must find, check that with independent readers, then run real indexers, SDKs and indexer code against it."],[2021,"**What it is.** Two bugs XDRProof found in other projects' code that nobody had reported, filed upstream, and a tested fix offered on a third that was already open."],[1996,"**What it is.** What building the corpus measured about stellar-core and the decoders: things the documentation doesn't say."],[2382,"**Where a value came from.** Each value carries labels for its source: text bytes, a possibly muxed address, a number a contract chose, the declared footprint, the authorization tree, an operation body, a diagnostic event. Sources are named SDK calls, conversions and field reads."],[2383,"**Where it is kept.** A sink is where a value is stored: a JSON- or db-tagged field, a map key, a set member, a builder's account. A map or set counts only when it outlives the function; a scratch map for de-duplication is not attribution."],[2089,"**Why it matters.** A reader that only looks at operations misses state that changed for other reasons, and its balances drift away from the ledger."],[1944,"**Why it matters.** A shape you can rebuild from scratch is one anyone can verify, and one that can be made again when the protocol changes."],[2419,"**Why it matters.** A test is only as good as its expected answer. Readers that cannot share a bug with the SDK under test keep the answers honest."],[2022,"**Why it matters.** A test kit earns trust by what it catches. Each report below comes with a figure, a reproduction anyone can run, and, where we could, a tested fix. Filing is done the way the projects ask: a duplicate search first, one issue per bug."],[2079,"**Why it matters.** A value that changes when it is converted is a wrong number in front of a user: an amount rounded, a map key lost, a signature that won't verify."],[2074,"**Why it matters.** An address is the key an indexer files everything under. When it is longer or stranger than a G… key, rows land under the wrong account, or the insert fails."],[2455,"**Why it matters.** An indexer that halts stops a wallet's history; one that stores a wrong balance is worse. Replay shows which ledgers do that before mainnet does."],[2109,"**Why it matters.** An SDK is the first thing to touch untrusted bytes. It must refuse bad input cleanly; crashing, or quietly accepting it, passes the problem on."],[1971,"**Why it matters.** Any tool, in any language, can load the corpus and know what each field means without reading XDRProof's code."],[2099,"**Why it matters.** Code tested on ordinary ledgers meets the unusual ones at upgrades and peaks: empty, huge, reordered or upgraded ledgers."],[2434,"**Why it matters.** Code that assumes a fixed order works on one run and breaks on the next. The corpus ships the rarer orderings, so that code fails in a test instead."],[1997,"**Why it matters.** Each one is a trap for anyone reading the ledger, and each is now a test in the corpus."],[2039,"**Why it matters.** Each one is pinned. If a fix regresses, or an old bug turns up in another SDK, the run fails."],[2203,"**Why it matters.** Each step is one `make` command, so any claim on this site can be re-checked."],[2094,"**Why it matters.** Events are the easiest way to follow contract activity, and the easiest to over-trust: some repeat, some carry deep data, some come from failed calls."],[2342,"**Why it matters.** Every number on this site comes from one of these commands, and each prints its result on your machine."],[2128,"**Why it matters.** Everything marked built here exists in the repository today and re-runs with one command, so you can check it instead of trusting it. What the grant would fund is listed separately."],[2498,"**Why it matters.** If the corpus were wrong, every test built on it would be wrong too. This runs in seconds, so it runs on every change."],[2058,"**Why it matters.** It is the corpus's blueprint: each entry targets hazards from this list, and each hazard says which check exercises it."],[2326,"**Why it matters.** It proves the corpus is reproducible: nothing in it depends on our machine, our timing or our luck."],[2307,"**Why it matters.** It turns \"we test the rare cases\" into a number. Every branch the corpus lacks has a written reason, with the stellar-core source line behind it."],[2228,"**Why it matters.** Most ledgers are ordinary, so the rare shapes go untested until one arrives in production, where it can stop an indexer or store a wrong balance. XDRProof lets every team meet them in a test first."],[8,"**Why it matters.** Nobody edits the corpus by hand, and the compiler refuses any fact that doesn't hold, so a typo can't become a wrong answer."],[1925,"**Why it matters.** Some shapes only happen on a live network, such as eviction at the per-ledger cap or a validator vote. These are the real bytes, not a reconstruction."],[2402,"**Why it matters.** The corpus grows by purpose, not by volume, so a team can run all of it without wading through duplicates."],[2181,"**Why it matters.** The corpus is exact about what an entry, a variant or a fact is. Knowing these words makes every other page quicker to read."],[2104,"**Why it matters.** The same transaction can be different bytes on another network or protocol. Code that assumes one of them breaks on the other."],[2378,"**Why it matters.** These three mistakes are how real indexers have halted or stored wrong data. The linter finds the code patterns themselves, whether or not a ledger has triggered them yet. On wallet-backend `main` it reports , all real code patterns and none reachable by a ledger today: one is hardening at today's limits, three sit in code Blend's contracts cannot reach. With XDRProof's two patches it reports ."],[2281,"**Why it matters.** When two SDKs read the same bytes differently, one of them is wrong, and every app built on it inherits the mistake. This finds it first."],[2084,"**Why it matters.** Whether a transaction succeeded, what it cost and what it changed are three different questions, and the ledger answers each in a different place."],[2253,"**Why it matters.** You don't have to trust any number on this site. `make verify` re-checks every entry on your own machine in seconds."],[1906,"**Why it matters.** Your code meets ordinary ledgers every day and these rarely, often for the first time in production. The corpus lets you meet them in a test."],[2166,"**Workflows** with actions pinned by SHA and read-only permissions: check, conformance, replay, determinism, golden agreement, an upstream watch that opens a pull request when stellar-xdr, stellar-core or an SDK tags a release, and release with checksums and provenance."],[2234,"**XDRProof turns each of those shapes into a test everyone can run.** One team's incident becomes every team's regression test, and one SDK's fix becomes a check on all of them."],[2010,"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM, refuses  corpus variants that stellar-core wrote (`depth limit exceeded`): 8 whose authorization tree is 245 levels deep, the deepest that applies, and 5 at 246, which core includes and traps. The package hard-codes `XDR_MAX_DEPTH = 500`, set to mirror the Soroban host's budget limit, and applies it to the whole `LedgerCloseMeta`, whose path down to the auth tree is deeper. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0, go-stellar-sdk v0.9.0, both Python decoders and native rs-stellar-xdr read them. This is finding F-1, reported as js-stellar-xdr-json#67; see Reported upstream."],[2025,"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, the WebAssembly build of rs-stellar-xdr, limits nesting to 500 levels for the whole value it decodes. stellar-core allows 500 per authorization entry, and the layers around a transaction add more, so the deepest trees core applies cannot be decoded once they are inside a transaction or a ledger. The corpus made them on a standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 with Soroban's resource limits raised for testing (`TESTING_SOROBAN_HIGH_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`), which the entry records."],[2059,"`caps/registry.json` is the blueprint for the corpus. Each **hazard** is a named way a reader of the Stellar ledger breaks. Some examples:"],[2007,"`fee_charged` is then the full declared maximum, with no refund change and no refund event. A reader that computes the fee as the charge minus the refund, or that expects a refund for every Soroban transaction, gets it wrong."],[2299,"`js@17.4.0` and `python-xdr@0.2.1` read every corpus variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does: the bytes, the SEP-0051 JSON and every expected fact."],[2356,"`make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."],[2357,"`make build` is not a check: it compiles `catalog/` into `entries/` from the runs of `make generate` and `mined/`, in minutes. See Catalog."],[1561,"`make conformance` runs a vector with the same kind of collision (a two-entry map whose keys both read as \"1\") through js-stellar-sdk builds before and after the fix. The recorded matrix shows 17.2.0 and 17.2.1 keeping 1 of 2 entries, and 17.3.0 rejecting the map."],[2315,"`make coverage` counts every arm in an entry's file, including arms from other transactions that happen to share its ledger. `make coverage-report` is stricter. An entry **reaches** an arm only when its own transaction (envelope, fee changes, apply meta) or its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys) contains it. Every arm no entry reaches is classified in `coverage/exclusions.json`, with the stellar-core source line that decides it. The run fails if an arm is in neither list, or in both."],[2004,"`make generate` re-measures this into `recipes/<lane>/profiles/`."],[2328,"`make generate` runs **10 pairs**, which is 20 fresh standalone cores, 6 at a time. There is no RPC, no network, and no keys or funds."],[1962,"`make generate` runs 10 configurations twice each: classic P30 on both passphrases, Soroban P30 and P28 on both passphrases, and the galexie and bare profiles at P30 mainnet for each lane. It checks that every shipped generated variant is reproduced. See Generate."],[2390,"`make lint-selftest` runs xplint on seeded fixtures, each modelled on real code and named in its fixture, then mutates the good cases."],[2159,"`make novelty` checks that no entry is redundant: over  atoms,  entries hold something no other entry does, and the other  differ by where the hazard sits."],[2158,"`make oracle` confirms each checkable value with two readers that share no code with any SDK: stellar-core's own C++ decoder and XDRProof's schema walker."],[1626,"`make replay` feeds testnet ledgers 4437356–4437359 to three real wallet-backend builds through a mock stellar-rpc:"],[1507,"`make replay` feeds this entry's **P28** variant to two wallet-backend builds through a mock stellar-rpc. Build `764a4f5b` **drops the trustline**: no TRUSTLINE/ADD state change is recorded. Build `c152783c`, \"record SAC-created trustline state changes\" (#734, the fix for #731), records it. See Replay."],[1936,"`make verify` also runs `tools/mined-verify.mjs`. Every file must match its hash, round-trip byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present. `make coverage` reports the union arms these ledgers reach: ** of **."],[18,"`make verify` and `make oracle` check the built entries like any other. `make novelty` then fails if the new entry adds nothing another entry doesn't already hold."],[1913,"`make verify` checks the schema, sizes and hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact. `make oracle` checks that each fact's values also appear in what stellar-core's own decoder and the schema walker read."],[2157,"`make verify` round-trips every variant, byte for byte, through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0 and checks every fact."],[1920,"`manifest.json` is generated from the entries, one row per variant. `make verify` fails if it is out of date."],[2504,"`manifest.json` is rebuilt from the entries. Without `--write`, verify fails if the committed file has drifted."],[1933,"`mined/` holds those as real `LedgerCloseMeta`, fetched from SDF's public data lake and located with BigQuery over the public Hubble dataset. The queries are in `mined/queries/`."],[2460,"`replay/lib/derive.mjs` reads the ledger with rs-stellar-xdr and lists the **probes**: the facts a correct indexer has stored after ingesting it. They come from the ledger alone."],[2461,"`replay/lib/mock-rpc.mjs` serves the ledger as stellar-rpc v30.0.1 would: `getLedgers`, `getHealth`, `getLatestLedger`, `getNetwork`, `getVersionInfo`, and `getLedgerEntries` from the state the ledger leaves behind. Pagination, page limits and validation errors follow stellar-rpc word for word."],[2505,"`tools/mined-verify.mjs` checks every mined ledger. It must match its hash and size, round-trip through the native reader, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present."],[2162,"`xplint` is a set of Go analyzers, one per mistake the RFP names. It writes text, JSON and SARIF for GitHub code scanning, and it fires before each upstream fix of these mistakes and goes quiet after. Linter"],[2484,"<Callout title=\"A new failure, a changed one and a fixed one all fail the run\">`replay/expectations/<adapter>@<target>.json` lists every variant that is not `handled`, with the exact probes that failed, the cause and the upstream issue. It also pins the set of variants judged (their count and a sha256 of their ids), so none can drop out of a run unnoticed.</Callout>"],[29,"<Callout title=\"Authoring aid\">`node tools/inspect.mjs <lane> <step> [variant]` reads a `make generate` run and prints the focus transaction's result and fee, and for each operation the kinds of its changes and its events.</Callout>"],[1937,"<Callout title=\"Network data, not entries\">These ledgers were written by SDF's Galexie with its default flags, so their metadata differs from a default node's (see Found while building). Most are not corpus entries: entries carry facts and hazards, and these carry findings. The catalog also compiles entries from them: entries so far, built from of these ledgers, the signed-payload signer among them.</Callout>"],[1560,"<Callout title=\"Not run yet\">The schema says SDK checks belong to the conformance runner, but no tool runs them yet. Today the runner uses its own vectors, below.</Callout>"],[2413,"<Callout title=\"Placement is a claim, not a measurement\">A placement is text the author wrote. So `make novelty` lists every entry told apart only by placement, and flags the ones wholly contained in one other entry: for each such pair, the facts must check a different thing.</Callout>"],[2429,"<Callout title=\"What it does not check\">It checks that a value is present in a reader's output, not that it sits at the fact's path; the path is checked by Verify.</Callout>"],[2000,"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"],[2441,"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"],[1914,"<File name=\"<id>.json\" />"],[1915,"<File name=\"entry.json\" />"],[1919,"<File name=\"entry.schema.json\" />"],[1918,"<File name=\"manifest.json\" />"],[1917,"<File name=\"p28-testnet-generated.xdr\" />"],[1916,"<File name=\"p30-mainnet-generated.xdr\" />"],[25,"| `at`                                     | Resolves to                        |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `tx.fee_processing`, `tx.post_apply_fee` | the fee and post-apply fee changes |"],[26,"| `at`                                 | Resolves to                         |\n| ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |\n| `op.meta`, `op.changes`, `op.events` | the focus operation inside the meta |"],[21,"| `at`             | Resolves to                       |\n| ---------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `tx.result_pair` | the result pair (hash and result) |"],[22,"| `at`             | Resolves to     |\n| ---------------- | --------------- |\n| `tx.fee_charged` | the fee charged |"],[23,"| `at`          | Resolves to                                                         |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.envelope` | the envelope in the transaction set, in any phase, stage or cluster |"],[20,"| `at`        | Resolves to                    |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `tx.result` | the focus transaction's result |"],[24,"| `at`      | Resolves to            |\n| --------- | ---------------------- |\n| `tx.meta` | the transaction's meta |"],[27,"| `at`     | Resolves to                                      |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ledger` | the whole ledger: header, upgrades, evicted keys |"],[2219,"| Against             | How                                                                                                       | Target      |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Indexer source code | `xplint` follows each value from where it came from to where it is kept, for the three ingestion mistakes | `make lint` |"],[2218,"| Against    | How                                                                                                          | Target        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| An indexer | real wallet-backend builds ingest every corpus ledger, one isolated session each, through a mock stellar-rpc | `make replay` |"],[2217,"| Against  | How                                                                                                                                                                             | Target             |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| The SDKs | builds of the JavaScript, WASM, Rust, Go and Python SDKs read every corpus variant, plus strkey, invalid-input and depth vectors;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions | `make conformance` |"],[2404,"| Atom                        | What counts                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Union arms and enum members | those reached by the entry's own transaction (its envelope and result meta) or by its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys), in any variant. Other transactions that share the ledger count for nothing |"],[2407,"| Atom       | What counts                                        |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Placements | each pair of a hazard and where in the XDR it sits |"],[2406,"| Atom      | What counts                       |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Sightings | each upstream issue it reproduces |"],[2405,"| Atom    | What counts                   |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Hazards | each hazard the entry targets |"],[2215,"| Check                                                                                  | Reader                             | Target                 |\n| -------------------------------------------------------